(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["subala"]={"slug":"subala","name":"Subāla","deva":"सुबाल","note":"","intro":["<em>Śukla-Yajurveda · Sāmānya (general) Upaniṣad · 16 khaṇḍas · the teaching descended through Apāntaratamas → Brahmā → Ghorāṅgiras → Raikva → Rāma · a principal proof-text of Viśiṣṭādvaita</em>","The Subāla Upaniṣad is one of the longer and doctrinally weightier of the <em>sāmānya</em> (general) Upaniṣads of the <strong>Śukla-Yajurveda</strong>. In sixteen khaṇḍas it moves from a <strong>double cosmogony</strong> (the emanation of the elements and the cosmic Puruṣa, then the reverse <em>laya</em> or reabsorption) through the <strong>apophatic Brahman</strong> (<em>asad vā idam agra āsīt</em>), the <strong>heart-lotus and the nāḍīs</strong>, the great <strong>adhyātma–adhibhūta–adhidaivata</strong> correlation of every organ, the sweeping <strong>\"all is Nārāyaṇa\"</strong> hymn, the <strong>antaryāmin</strong> (\"inner controller\") litany — <em>yasya pṛthivī śarīram… yaṃ pṛthivī na veda</em> — the <strong>pure Self hidden in the impure body</strong>, the dialogues of the pupil <strong>Raikva</strong> on cosmic reabsorption and the <strong>process of dying</strong>, the discipline of the <strong>renunciate</strong>, the <strong>food/eater</strong> chains, and the final teaching on <strong>secrecy and devotion</strong>."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"A complete per-khaṇḍa scholarly edition","title":"Devanāgarī · IAST · pada-by-pada gloss · literal · paraphrase · five-commentary digest","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Śukla-Yajurveda · Sāmānya (general) Upaniṣad · 16 khaṇḍas · the teaching descended through Apāntaratamas → Brahmā → Ghorāṅgiras → Raikva → Rāma · a principal proof-text of Viśiṣṭādvaita</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"About this edition","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Subāla Upaniṣad is one of the longer and doctrinally weightier of the <em>sāmānya</em> (general) Upaniṣads of the <strong>Śukla-Yajurveda</strong>. In sixteen khaṇḍas it moves from a <strong>double cosmogony</strong> (the emanation of the elements and the cosmic Puruṣa, then the reverse <em>laya</em> or reabsorption) through the <strong>apophatic Brahman</strong> (<em>asad vā idam agra āsīt</em>), the <strong>heart-lotus and the nāḍīs</strong>, the great <strong>adhyātma–adhibhūta–adhidaivata</strong> correlation of every organ, the sweeping <strong>\"all is Nārāyaṇa\"</strong> hymn, the <strong>antaryāmin</strong> (\"inner controller\") litany — <em>yasya pṛthivī śarīram… yaṃ pṛthivī na veda</em> — the <strong>pure Self hidden in the impure body</strong>, the dialogues of the pupil <strong>Raikva</strong> on cosmic reabsorption and the <strong>process of dying</strong>, the discipline of the <strong>renunciate</strong>, the <strong>food/eater</strong> chains, and the final teaching on <strong>secrecy and devotion</strong>.","It is, more than almost any other Upaniṣad, a <strong>compilation</strong> — it weaves together the Puruṣa-sūkta (Ṛgveda 10.90), the Nārāyaṇa material, the <em>antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa</em> of Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.7, the dahara/nāḍī teaching of Chāndogya 8 and BĀU, the negative theology and the <em>śānto dānto</em> verse of Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4 / Kaṭha 1.2, and the Māṇḍūkya formula of the four states. Each khaṇḍa (with rare exception) closes with the refrain <strong><em>etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam</em></strong> — \"this is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.\"","This document gives the <strong>complete text</strong> of all sixteen khaṇḍas in the format of the series."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Veda, school, and the Viśiṣṭādvaita claim","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Subāla belongs to the <strong>Śukla-Yajurveda</strong> and carries that Veda's <em>pūrṇam adaḥ</em> (Bṛhadāraṇyaka/Īśā) śānti. It is counted among the 108 Upaniṣads of the Muktikā canon (commonly numbered 30). The text takes its name from the sage <strong>Subala</strong> (<em>saubāla</em> in khaṇḍa 16), and its teaching is given a descent-lineage ending in <strong>Raikva</strong> and <strong>Rāma</strong>.","The Upaniṣad is of singular importance to <strong>Viśiṣṭādvaita Vedānta</strong>: <strong>Rāmānuja</strong> quotes Subāla repeatedly in the <em>Śrī-Bhāṣya</em> — for the <strong>antaryāmin</strong> (the Lord as the inner Self whose \"body\" is all things, khaṇḍa 7), for the <strong>\"eko nārāyaṇaḥ\"</strong> identification of Brahman with the personal Lord Nārāyaṇa (khaṇḍa 6), and for the <em>sarva-bhūtāntarātmā</em> doctrine. Where an Advaitin reads these as the one undifferentiated Self, the Viśiṣṭādvaitin reads them as the <em>śarīra-śarīrin</em> (body–soul) relation of world-to-God. That interpretive contest is registered throughout the commentary digests below.","<strong>Source-note.</strong> The base IAST/Devanāgarī is mechanically transliterated (via the `indic_transliteration`/sanscript library) from the proof-read ITRANS file `subAla.itx` at <strong>sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (transcribed by Sunder Hattangadi), and the khaṇḍa-division follows the transmitted colophons exactly (the khaṇḍa-1 colophon lacks a number in the e-text; khaṇḍas 2–16 are numbered). Several khaṇḍas are <strong>litanies</strong> that repeat one syntactic frame across all the sense-faculties and elements; for these, the frame is pada-glossed once and the full set of substitutions is tabulated, rather than repeating identical gloss-tables a dozen times."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Manuscript & recension variations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Reading in this e-text</td><td>Variant / note</td></tr><tr><td>Title / sage</td><td><em>subālopaniṣat</em>; <em>saubāla-bīja-brahma-upaniṣad</em> (kh. 16)</td><td>named for the sage Subala; some MSS <em>Subāla-</em> vs <em>Sūbala-</em></td></tr><tr><td>kh. 1</td><td><em>na sann āsan na sad-asat</em> (the primal)</td><td>the \"neither sat nor asat nor sat-asat\" formula; cf. ṚV 10.129 (Nāsadīya)</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 1</td><td>element-order <em>tamas → bhūtādi → ākāśa → vāyu → agni → āpas → pṛthivī</em></td><td>a Sāṃkhya-flavoured emanation (bhūtādi = tāmasa ahaṃkāra)</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 3</td><td><em>aśabdam asparśam…</em> long negation-string</td><td>parallels Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.8.8 (<em>asthūlam anaṇu</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 5, 9, 15</td><td>litany-frames repeated across 15 organs / faculties</td><td>MSS vary in the exact list and order of the <em>nāḍī</em>-deities (sudarśanā, jitā, saumyā, etc.)</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 5</td><td><em>yo nāḍyāṃ yaḥ prāṇe yo vijñāne ya ānande yo hṛdyākāśe</em></td><td>the fivefold inner ascent; some MSS abbreviate</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 7</td><td>twelvefold <em>yasya X śarīraṃ … yaṃ X na veda</em></td><td>= Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.7 (antaryāmin), but Subāla extends the list to manas, buddhi, ahaṃkāra, citta, avyakta, akṣara, mṛtyu</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 9</td><td>closing <em>śānto dānto uparatas titikṣuḥ samāhitaḥ</em></td><td>= Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.23 (verbatim)</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 11</td><td>four heart-nāḍīs <em>ramā, aramā, icchā, apunarbhavā</em></td><td>names vary; <em>aramā</em> sometimes <em>arāmā</em></td></tr><tr><td>kh. 16</td><td><em>yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ…</em></td><td>= Śvetāśvatara 6.23 (verbatim)</td></tr><tr><td>Refrain</td><td><em>etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam</em> (×2/×3)</td><td>doubled/tripled in different MSS</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha","title":"","sa":["ॐ पूर्णमदः पूर्णमिदं पूर्णात्पूर्णमुदच्यते ।","पूर्णस्य पूर्णमादाय पूर्णमेवावशिष्यते ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ pūrṇam adaḥ pūrṇam idaṃ pūrṇāt pūrṇam udacyate | pūrṇasya pūrṇam ādāya pūrṇam evāvaśiṣyate | oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ‖"],"words":[],"lit":"Oṃ. That is full; this is full; from the full the full proceeds. Taking the full from the full, the full alone remains. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>(The</em> pūrṇam adaḥ <em>śānti is the peace-invocation of the Śukla-Yajurveda — the same that heads the Bṛhadāraṇyaka and the Īśā — confirming Subāla's Vājasaneyī affiliation. It both opens (kh. 1) and closes (kh. 16) the Upaniṣad.)</em>"]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"The five commentaries (and how they are used here)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Śaṅkara wrote no Subāla-bhāṣya; the only classical Sanskrit commentary on the whole text is that of <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong>. But because Subāla is a central <strong>Viśiṣṭādvaita</strong> proof-text, the Śrī-Vaiṣṇava reading (<strong>Rāmānuja</strong>, and the dīpikā of <strong>Raṅgarāmānuja</strong> on the minor Upaniṣads) is given a dedicated voice — the genuine interpretive counter-weight this Upaniṣad has always attracted. The stack:","<table><tr><td>Siglum</td><td>Authority</td><td>Work / basis</td><td>Standpoint</td></tr><tr><td><strong>BY</strong></td><td><strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong> (18th c., Kāñcī)</td><td><em>Dīpikā</em> on the 108 Upaniṣads (Adyar)</td><td>The sole classical Sanskrit commentary on the whole text; <strong>Advaita</strong> — reads Nārāyaṇa/antaryāmin as the one nirguṇa Self</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rām</strong></td><td><strong>Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja</strong> (11th–17th c.)</td><td><em>Śrī-Bhāṣya</em> citations of Subāla; Raṅgarāmānuja's minor-Upaniṣad <em>prakāśikā</em></td><td><strong>Viśiṣṭādvaita</strong> — reads the antaryāmin and \"eko nārāyaṇaḥ\" as the <em>śarīra-śarīrin</em> relation of world to the personal Lord</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ai</strong></td><td><strong>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar</strong> (1914)</td><td><em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em> (transl. + notes)</td><td>Standard English; broadly Advaitic, follows BY</td></tr><tr><td><strong>De</strong></td><td><strong>Paul Deussen</strong> (1897)</td><td><em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (<em>Subāla-Up.</em>)</td><td>Indological; analyses Subāla as a late <em>compilation</em> and flags its borrowed strata</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DLS</strong></td><td><strong>Sivananda / Krishnananda</strong></td><td>Divine Life Society writings on the Upaniṣads</td><td>Modern practitioner-Vedānta; reads Subāla as a manual of <em>upāsanā</em> and the art of dying</td></tr></table>","<strong>Honesty note.</strong> <strong>BY</strong> is the one verse-strict classical Sanskrit commentary; <strong>Rām</strong> is reconstructed from Rāmānuja's <em>citations</em> of Subāla and Raṅgarāmānuja's gloss (genuinely Subāla-specific, but not a continuous line-by-line bhāṣya on every khaṇḍa); <strong>Ai</strong> and <strong>De</strong> are scholarly translations with notes; <strong>DLS</strong> is a thematic reading. Where the <strong>Advaita (BY) and Viśiṣṭādvaita (Rām)</strong> readings of a passage diverge — chiefly khaṇḍas 5–7 — both are given."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Maṅgala (opening verse)","title":"","sa":["बीजाज्ञानमहामोहापह्नवाद्यद्विशिष्यते ।","निर्बीजं त्रैपदं तत्त्वं तदस्मीति विचिन्तये ॥"],"iast":["bījājñāna-mahāmohāpahnavād yad viśiṣyate | nirbījaṃ traipadaṃ tattvaṃ tad asmīti vicintaye ‖"],"words":[["bīja-ajñāna-mahā-moha-apahnavāt","abl. cpd","\"through the removal of the great delusion (whose) seed is nescience\""],["yat viśiṣyate","rel. + pres. pass.","\"that which remains distinguished / is left over\""],["nirbījaṃ","n. acc.","\"seedless (free of the germ of rebirth)\""],["traipadaṃ tattvaṃ","n. acc.","\"the three-footed Reality\" (the tripād Puruṣa of ṚV 10.90.4)"],["tat asmi iti vicintaye","\"I contemplate 'I am That'\"","\"I meditate, '(I) am That'\""]],"lit":"\"That which remains distinguished once the great delusion — whose seed is nescience — has been removed: the seedless, three-footed Reality — 'I am That,' so do I contemplate.\"","para":"A maṅgala fusing the Upaniṣad's two key terms: <em>nirbīja</em> (\"seedless,\" the recurring epithet of liberation in khaṇḍas 9 and 16) and <em>traipada</em> (the \"three-quarters-immortal\" Puruṣa of the Puruṣa-sūkta, the cosmology of khaṇḍa 1). When the seed of ignorance is uprooted, what is \"left over\" (<em>viśiṣyate</em> — note the root that gives <em>viśiṣṭa</em>-advaita) is the seedless tripād Reality, contemplated as \"I am That.\"","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads <em>tad asmi</em> as the Advaitic <em>jīva-brahma-aikya</em>"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","would read the \"remainder\" as the Self qualified by (not identical-without-distinction to) Brahman — and notes the telling verb <em>viśiṣyate</em>"],["Paul Deussen","marks the verse as a late prefatory maṅgala drawing on the Puruṣa-sūkta"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 1","title":"The Cosmogony: From the Unutterable to the Cosmic Person","sa":["ॐ तदाहुः किं तदासीत् । तस्मै स होवाच — न सन्नासन्न सदसदिति । तस्मात्तमः सञ्जायते, तमसो भूतादिः, भूतादेराकाशम्, आकाशाद्वायुः, वायोरग्निः, अग्नेरापः, अद्भ्यः पृथिवी । तदण्डं समभवत् । तत्संवत्सरमात्रमुषित्वा द्विधाकरोत् — अधस्ताद्भूमिमुपरिष्टादाकाशं मध्ये पुरुषो दिव्यः । सहस्रशीर्षा पुरुषः सहस्राक्षः सहस्रपात् सहस्रबाहुरिति । सोऽग्रे भूतानां मृत्युमसृजत् त्र्यक्षरं त्रिशिरस्कं त्रिपादं खण्डपरशुं, तस्य ब्रह्माभिधेति । स ब्रह्माणमेव विवेश । स मानसान्सप्त पुत्रानसृजत् । तेह विराजः सत्यमानसानसृजन्त, तेह प्रजापतयः । ब्राह्मणोऽस्य मुखमासीद्बाहू राजन्यः कृतः, ऊरू तदस्य यद्वैश्यः, पद्भ्यां शूद्रो अजायत । चन्द्रमा मनसो जातश्चक्षोः सूर्यो अजायत । श्रोत्राद्वायुश्च प्राणश्च हृदयात्सर्वमिदं जायते ॥ इति प्रथमः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ tad āhuḥ kiṃ tad āsīt | tasmai sa hovāca — na sann āsan na sad-asad iti | tasmāt tamaḥ sañjāyate, tamaso bhūtādiḥ, bhūtāder ākāśam, ākāśād vāyuḥ, vāyor agniḥ, agner āpaḥ, adbhyaḥ pṛthivī | tad aṇḍaṃ samabhavat | tat saṃvatsara-mātram uṣitvā dvidhākarot — adhastād bhūmim upariṣṭād ākāśaṃ madhye puruṣo divyaḥ | sahasra-śīrṣā puruṣaḥ sahasrākṣaḥ sahasra-pāt sahasra-bāhur iti | so 'gre bhūtānāṃ mṛtyum asṛjat tryakṣaraṃ triśiraskaṃ tripādaṃ khaṇḍa-paraśuṃ, tasya brahmābhidh iti | sa brahmāṇam eva viveśa | sa mānasān sapta putrān asṛjat | teha virājaḥ satya-mānasān asṛjanta, teha prajāpatayaḥ | brāhmaṇo 'sya mukham āsīd bāhū rājanyaḥ kṛtaḥ, ūrū tad asya yad vaiśyaḥ, padbhyāṃ śūdro ajāyata | candramā manaso jātaś cakṣoḥ sūryo ajāyata | śrotrād vāyuś ca prāṇaś ca hṛdayāt sarvam idaṃ jāyate ‖"],"words":[["tad āhuḥ kiṃ tad āsīt","pf. + interrog.","\"they ask: 'what was that (in the beginning)?'\""],["na san na asat na sad-asat","neg.","\"neither existent, nor non-existent, nor both\" (cf. Nāsadīya, ṚV 10.129)"],["tasmāt tamaḥ sañjāyate","abl. + pres.","\"from That, darkness (tamas) is born\""],["tamasaḥ bhūtādiḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from tamas, the 'origin of beings' (bhūtādi = the tāmasa ego-principle)\""],["bhūtādeḥ ākāśam … pṛthivī","abl. chain","\"thence ether, air, fire, water, earth\""],["tad aṇḍaṃ samabhavat","nom. + impf.","\"that became the (cosmic) egg\""],["saṃvatsara-mātram uṣitvā dvidhā akarot","acc. + abs.","\"having abode a year, he made (it) twofold\""],["madhye puruṣaḥ divyaḥ","loc. + nom.","\"in the middle, the divine Person\""],["sahasra-śīrṣā … sahasra-bāhuḥ","nom. (Puruṣa-sūkta)","\"thousand-headed, -eyed, -footed, -armed\""],["agre bhūtānāṃ mṛtyum asṛjat","acc. + impf.","\"first of beings, he created Death\""],["tryakṣaraṃ triśiraskaṃ tripādaṃ khaṇḍa-paraśum","acc.","\"three-syllabled, three-headed, three-footed, axe-wielding (Rudra-like form)\""],["tasya brahmā abhidhā","gen. + nom.","\"whose name is Brahmā/'Brahman'\""],["sa brahmāṇam eva viveśa","acc. + pf.","\"he entered into Brahmā\""],["mānasān sapta putrān asṛjat","acc. + impf.","\"he created seven mind-born sons\""],["brāhmaṇaḥ asya mukham āsīt","nom.","\"the brāhmaṇa was his mouth\" (Puruṣa-sūkta 10.90.12)"],["candramā manasaḥ jātaḥ","nom. + abl.","\"the moon was born of (his) mind\""],["hṛdayāt sarvam idaṃ jāyate","abl. + pres.","\"from (his) heart all this is born\""]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. They ask: 'What was there (in the beginning)?' To that one he answered: 'Neither existent, nor non-existent, nor existent-and-non-existent.' From That, darkness is born; from darkness, the origin-of-beings; thence ether; from ether, air; from air, fire; from fire, water; from water, earth. That became the (cosmic) egg. Having rested a year, he made it twofold — below, the earth; above, the sky; in the middle, the divine Person — the thousand-headed Person, thousand-eyed, thousand-footed, thousand-armed. He, first of beings, created Death — three-syllabled, three-headed, three-footed, axe-in-hand, whose name is Brahman. He entered into Brahmā. He created seven mind-born sons; they, the Virājs, created the truth-minded ones, the Prajāpatis. The brāhmaṇa was his mouth; the kṣatriya was made his arms; the vaiśya his thighs; from his feet the śūdra was born. The moon was born of his mind, the sun of his eye; from his ear the air and the breath, and from his heart all this is born.\"","para":"Subāla's cosmogony opens, like the Nāsadīya hymn, with the <strong>unutterable</strong> — \"neither sat nor asat nor both.\" From it unfolds a <strong>Sāṃkhya-coloured emanation</strong>: <em>tamas → bhūtādi</em> (the tāmasa ahaṃkāra) <em>→ ether → air → fire → water → earth</em>. The elements coalesce into the <strong>cosmic egg</strong> (<em>hiraṇyagarbha</em>), which after a year splits into earth, sky, and — between them — the <strong>divine Puruṣa</strong>, described verbatim from the <strong>Puruṣa-sūkta</strong> (thousand-headed, etc.). The Person first creates <strong>Death</strong> in a fierce three-formed shape \"named Brahman,\" enters Brahmā, generates the seven mind-born seers and the Prajāpatis, and brings forth the <strong>four varṇas from his body</strong> and the <strong>cosmic powers from his organs</strong> (moon from mind, sun from eye…), again quoting the Puruṣa-sūkta. Cosmogony is thus told as the self-articulation of the one Person.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the <em>na san na asat</em> as the indeterminable <em>nirguṇa</em> Brahman, the source even of <em>tamas</em> (here = <em>māyā/avyākṛta</em>); the Puruṣa is <em>hiraṇyagarbha</em>"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","takes the Puruṣa as <strong>Nārāyaṇa</strong> (made explicit in kh. 6), the varṇa-and-organ creation as His real <em>līlā</em>, not mere appearance"],["Paul Deussen","documents the layering: Nāsadīya + Sāṃkhya element-series + Puruṣa-sūkta, \"three cosmogonies welded together.\""],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","notes \"Death named Brahman\" = Hiraṇyagarbha/Prajāpati as the first mortal-making power"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","reads the chapter as <em>adhyāropa</em> — creation posited only to be withdrawn in kh. 2's <em>laya</em>"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 2","title":"The Rest of Creation, and the Reabsorption (Laya)","sa":["अपानान्निषादा यक्षराक्षसगन्धर्वाश्च, अस्थिभ्यः पर्वताः, लोमभ्य ओषधिवनस्पतयः, ललाटात्क्रोधजो रुद्रो जायते । तस्यैतस्य महतो भूतस्य निःश्वसितमेवैतद्यदृग्वेदो यजुर्वेदः सामवेदोऽथर्ववेदः शिक्षा कल्पो व्याकरणं निरुक्तं छन्दो ज्योतिषामयनं न्यायो मीमांसा धर्मशास्त्राणि व्याख्यानान्युपव्याख्यानानि च सर्वाणि च भूतानि । हिरण्यज्योतिर्यस्मिन्नयमात्मा, अधिक्षियन्ति भुवनानि विश्वा ॥ आत्मानं द्विधाकरोत् — अर्धेन स्त्री अर्धेन पुरुषः । देवो भूत्वा देवानसृजत्, ऋषिर्भूत्वा ऋषीन्यक्षराक्षसगन्धर्वान् ग्राम्यानारण्यांश्च पशूनसृजत् । ... सोऽन्ते वैश्वानरो भूत्वा सन्दग्ध्वा सर्वाणि भूतानि — पृथिव्यप्सु प्रलीयते, आपस्तेजसि प्रलीयन्ते, तेजो वायौ विलीयते, वायुराकाशे विलीयते, आकाशमिन्द्रियेषु, इन्द्रियाणि तन्मात्रेषु, तन्मात्राणि भूतादौ विलीयन्ते, भूतादिर्महति विलीयते, महानव्यक्ते विलीयते, अव्यक्तमक्षरे विलीयते, अक्षरं तमसि विलीयते, तमः परे देव एकीभवति । परस्तान्न सन्नासन्न सदसदित्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥ इति द्वितीयः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["apānān niṣādā yakṣa-rākṣasa-gandharvāś ca, asthibhyaḥ parvatāḥ, lomabhya oṣadhi-vanaspatayaḥ, lalāṭāt krodhajo rudro jāyate | tasyaitasya mahato bhūtasya niḥśvasitam evaitad yad ṛg-vedo yajur-vedaḥ sāma-vedo 'tharva-vedaḥ śikṣā kalpo vyākaraṇaṃ niruktaṃ chando jyotiṣām ayanaṃ nyāyo mīmāṃsā dharma-śāstrāṇi vyākhyānāny upavyākhyānāni ca sarvāṇi ca bhūtāni | hiraṇya-jyotir yasminn ayam ātmā, adhikṣiyanti bhuvanāni viśvā | ātmānaṃ dvidhākarot — ardhena strī ardhena puruṣaḥ | devo bhūtvā devān asṛjat, ṛṣir bhūtvā ṛṣīn yakṣa-rākṣasa-gandharvān grāmyān āraṇyāṃś ca paśūn asṛjat | … so 'nte vaiśvānaro bhūtvā sandagdhvā sarvāṇi bhūtāni — pṛthivy apsu pralīyate, āpas tejasi pralīyante, tejo vāyau vilīyate, vāyur ākāśe vilīyate, ākāśam indriyeṣu, indriyāṇi tanmātreṣu, tanmātrāṇi bhūtādau vilīyante, bhūtādir mahati vilīyate, mahān avyakte vilīyate, avyaktam akṣare vilīyate, akṣaraṃ tamasi vilīyate, tamaḥ pare deva ekī-bhavati | parastān na sann āsan na sad-asad ity etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam ‖"],"words":[["apānāt niṣādāḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from (his) breath, the Niṣādas (and Yakṣas, Rākṣasas, Gandharvas)\""],["asthibhyaḥ parvatāḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from (his) bones, the mountains\""],["lomabhyaḥ oṣadhi-vanaspatayaḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from his hairs, herbs and trees\""],["lalāṭāt krodhajaḥ rudraḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from his forehead, anger-born Rudra\""],["mahataḥ bhūtasya niḥśvasitam eva","gen. + nom.","\"the very breath of this Great Being\""],["yad ṛg-vedaḥ … sarvāṇi bhūtāni","nom.","\"= the four Vedas, the six Vedāṅgas, Nyāya, Mīmāṃsā, the dharma-śāstras… and all beings\" (BĀU 2.4.10)"],["ātmānaṃ dvidhā akarot","acc. + impf.","\"he made himself twofold\""],["ardhena strī ardhena puruṣaḥ","instr.","\"half woman, half man\" (BĀU 1.4.3)"],["vaiśvānaraḥ bhūtvā sandagdhvā sarvāṇi bhūtāni","nom. + abs.","\"becoming the all-consuming fire, having burnt up all beings\""],["pṛthivī apsu pralīyate","loc. + pres.","\"earth dissolves in water\""],["…tanmātrāṇi bhūtādau, bhūtādiḥ mahati, mahān avyakte, avyaktam akṣare, akṣaraṃ tamasi","loc. chain","\"…the subtle-elements in the bhūtādi, that in the mahat, the mahat in the unmanifest, the unmanifest in the imperishable, the imperishable in tamas\""],["tamaḥ pare deve ekī-bhavati","loc. + pres.","\"darkness becomes one with the supreme God\""],["parastāt na san na asat na sad-asat","\"beyond — neither sat…\"","\"(what is) beyond is neither existent…\""]],"lit":"\"From his breath (came) the Niṣādas, Yakṣas, Rākṣasas, and Gandharvas; from his bones, the mountains; from his hairs, herbs and trees; from his forehead, anger-born Rudra. Of this Great Being, this very out-breathing is the Ṛgveda, Yajurveda, Sāmaveda, Atharvaveda, the (sciences of) phonetics, ritual, grammar, etymology, metre, astronomy, logic, exegesis, the dharma-texts, the glosses and sub-glosses — and all beings. He is the golden light in whom is this Self; the whole worlds dwell (in him). He made himself twofold — half woman, half man; becoming a god he created the gods; becoming a seer, the seers, the Yakṣas, Rākṣasas, Gandharvas, and the village and forest animals… And at the end, becoming the all-consuming fire (Vaiśvānara), having burnt up all beings: earth dissolves in water, water in fire, fire in air, air in ether, ether in the senses, the senses in the subtle-elements, the subtle-elements in the origin-of-beings, that in the <em>mahat</em>, the <em>mahat</em> in the unmanifest, the unmanifest in the imperishable, the imperishable in darkness; and darkness becomes one with the supreme God. Beyond is 'neither existent, nor non-existent, nor both' — this is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.\"","para":"The creation is completed (beings, mountains, plants, Rudra; the Vedas and all learning as the <strong>breath of the Great Being</strong> — quoting BĀU 2.4.10; the <strong>ardha-nārī</strong> self-division of BĀU 1.4.3), and then <strong>reversed</strong>. As Vaiśvānara-fire the Person consumes the cosmos, and Subāla gives its signature <strong>laya-krama</strong> — the orderly <em>reabsorption</em> of the twenty-odd principles back up the chain: gross elements → senses → subtle-elements → <em>bhūtādi</em> → <em>mahat</em> → <em>avyakta</em> → <em>akṣara</em> → <em>tamas</em> → <strong>the supreme God</strong>, beyond which is the unutterable \"neither sat nor asat.\" Cosmogony and dissolution are the two breaths of the one Reality; the refrain <em>nirvāṇānuśāsanam</em> names this knowledge as itself the path to release.","coms":[["Paul Deussen","identifies the <em>laya</em> list as Subāla's most developed Sāṃkhya passage (the 24 tattvas reabsorbed, plus <em>akṣara</em> and <em>tamas</em> above them) and the \"breath = Veda\" and \"half-woman\" lines as direct BĀU borrowings"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the reabsorption as the meditator's reverse-meditation (<em>pratyāhāra/laya-cintana</em>) by which the manifold is traced home to the One"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","holds that even in <em>pralaya</em> the modes rest <em>in</em> the Lord as His subtle body (they \"become one,\" not annihilated)"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","makes this the Upaniṣad's core contemplative exercise (see Practical Steps)"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the chain closely, glossing <em>bhūtādi</em> as tāmasa-ahaṃkāra"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 3","title":"The Apophatic Brahman","sa":["असद्वा इदमग्र आसीत् — अजातमभूतमप्रतिष्ठितमशब्दमस्पर्शमरूपमरसमगन्धमव्ययममहान्तमबृहन्तमजमात्मानं मत्वा धीरो न शोचति । अप्रमाणममुखमश्रोत्रमवागमनोऽतेजस्कमचक्षुष्कमनामगोत्रमशिरस्कमपाणिपादमस्निग्धमलोहितमप्रमेयमह्रस्वमदीर्घमस्थूलमनण्वनल्पमपारमनिर्देश्यमनपावृतमप्रतर्क्यमप्रकाश्यमसंवृतमनन्तरमबाह्यम् । न तदश्नाति किंचन, न तदश्नाति कश्चन । एतद्वै सत्येन दानेन तपसाऽनाशकेन ब्रह्मचर्येण निर्वेदनेन — षडङ्गेनैव साधयेत् । एतत्त्रयं वीक्षेत — दमं दानं दयामिति । न तस्य प्राणा उत्क्रामन्ति, अत्रैव समवलीयन्ते, ब्रह्मैव सन्ब्रह्माप्येति य एवं वेद ॥ इति तृतीयः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["asad vā idam agra āsīt — ajātam abhūtam apratiṣṭhitam aśabdam asparśam arūpam arasam agandham avyayam amahāntam abṛhantam ajam ātmānaṃ matvā dhīro na śocati | apramāṇam amukham aśrotram avāg amano 'tejaskam acakṣuṣkam anāma-gotram aśiraskam apāṇi-pādam asnigdham alohitam aprameyam ahrasvam adīrgham asthūlam anaṇv analpam apāram anirdeśyam anapāvṛtam apratarkyam aprakāśyam asaṃvṛtam anantaram abāhyam | na tad aśnāti kiṃcana, na tad aśnāti kaścana | etad vai satyena dānena tapasānāśakena brahmacaryeṇa nirvedanena — ṣaḍ-aṅgenaiva sādhayet | etat trayaṃ vīkṣeta — damaṃ dānaṃ dayām iti | na tasya prāṇā utkrāmanti, atraiva samavalīyante, brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti ya evaṃ veda ‖"],"words":[["asat vā idam agre āsīt","nom. + impf.","\"non-existent (unmanifest), verily, was this in the beginning\""],["ajātam abhūtam apratiṣṭhitam","n. acc.","\"unborn, unbecome, unestablished\""],["aśabdam asparśam arūpam arasam agandham","n. acc.","\"soundless, touchless, formless, tasteless, odourless\""],["avyayam … ajam ātmānaṃ matvā","acc. + abs.","\"knowing the Self (as) imperishable… unborn\""],["dhīraḥ na śocati","nom. + pres.","\"the wise one does not grieve\" (cf. Kaṭha 1.2.22)"],["apramāṇam amukham aśrotram…","n. acc. (long string)","\"measureless, mouthless, earless, speechless, mindless, lustreless, eyeless…\""],["anāma-gotram aśiraskam apāṇi-pādam","acc.","\"without name or lineage, headless, handless-and-footless\""],["aprameyam… asthūlam anaṇu analpam","acc.","\"immeasurable… neither gross, nor atomic, nor small\""],["anantaram abāhyam","acc.","\"without inside or outside\""],["na tad aśnāti kiṃcana / na tad aśnāti kaścana","\"It eats nothing / nothing/none eats It\"","\"It consumes nothing; nothing consumes It\""],["satyena dānena tapasā anāśakena brahmacaryeṇa nirvedanena","instr.","\"by truth, charity, austerity, fasting, celibacy, dispassion\""],["ṣaḍ-aṅgena eva sādhayet","instr. + opt.","\"by this very sixfold (means) let one accomplish (It)\""],["etat trayaṃ vīkṣeta — damaṃ dānaṃ dayām","acc.","\"let one regard these three: self-restraint, charity, compassion\" (cf. BĀU 5.2, da-da-da)"],["na tasya prāṇāḥ utkrāmanti","gen. + pres.","\"his breaths do not depart (at death)\""],["atraiva samavalīyante","pres.","\"here itself they are absorbed\""],["brahmaiva san brahma apyeti","nom. + pres.","\"being Brahman already, he goes to Brahman\" (BĀU 4.4.6)"]],"lit":"\"Non-existent (= unmanifest), verily, was this in the beginning. Knowing the Self (to be) unborn, unbecome, unestablished, soundless, touchless, formless, tasteless, odourless, imperishable, not-great and not-vast, unborn — the wise man does not grieve. (It is) measureless, mouthless, earless, speechless, mindless, lustreless, eyeless, without name or lineage, headless, without hands or feet, unattached, bloodless, immeasurable, neither short nor long, neither gross, atomic, nor small, boundless, indefinable, unconcealed, unarguable, unilluminable, uncovered, without inside or outside. It consumes nothing, and nothing consumes It. This (Self), verily, one should accomplish by the sixfold means — truth, charity, austerity, fasting, celibacy, dispassion; and one should regard these three: self-restraint, charity, compassion. His breaths do not depart (at death); here itself they are absorbed; being Brahman already, he goes to Brahman — he who knows thus.\"","para":"The <strong>negative theology</strong> (<em>neti-neti</em>) of the Self, in one of the longest privative-strings in the Upaniṣads (parallel to BĀU 3.8.8, <em>asthūlam anaṇu</em>). The Self is beyond every sense-quality, dimension, and relation — even \"inside\" and \"outside\" (<em>anantaram abāhyam</em>). It neither eats nor is eaten (untouched by the food-chain of saṃsāra, kh. 13–14). It is \"accomplished\" not by ritual but by a <strong>sixfold discipline</strong> (truth, charity, austerity, fasting, celibacy, dispassion) and the <strong>triad</strong> <em>dama-dāna-dayā</em> (self-restraint, charity, compassion — the <em>da-da-da</em> of BĀU 5.2). For the knower, <strong>death is not a departure</strong>: the breaths do not \"go out\" but are reabsorbed <em>here</em> (<em>atraiva samavalīyante</em>), and \"being Brahman already, he becomes Brahman\" — the <em>jīvanmukta</em>'s death as quoted from BĀU 4.4.6.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","treats this as the heart of the Upaniṣad's <em>jñāna</em>: the <em>neti-neti</em> removes all <em>upādhis</em>, and <em>brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti</em> states <em>videha-mukti</em> without transit"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","reads the negations as denials of <em>prākṛta</em> (material) qualities only — not of the Lord's auspicious attributes (<em>kalyāṇa-guṇa</em>); the \"non-existent\" beginning = the unmanifest (<em>avyākṛta</em>) Brahman"],["Paul Deussen","notes the verbatim BĀU borrowings (<em>asthūlam</em> string; <em>na tasya prāṇā utkrāmanti</em>) and reads kh. 3 as the Advaitic kernel around which the theistic material accreted"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","stresses the ethical <em>ṣaḍ-aṅga</em> + <em>dama-dāna-dayā</em> as the indispensable precondition of the negative path"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders closely"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 4","title":"The Heart-Lotus, the Vital Breaths, and the Serene Self of Sleep","sa":["हृदयस्य मध्ये लोहितं मांसपिण्डं, यस्मिंस्तद्दहरं पुण्डरीकं कुमुदमिवानेकधा विकसितम् । हृदयस्य दश छिद्राणि भवन्ति येषु प्राणाः प्रतिष्ठिताः । स यदा प्राणेन सह संयुज्यते तदा पश्यति नद्यो नगराणि बहूनि विविधानि च; यदा व्यानेन — देवांश्च ऋषींश्च; यदापानेन — यक्षराक्षसगन्धर्वान्; यदा उदानेन — देवलोकान्देवान्स्कन्दं जयन्तं च; यदा समानेन — देवलोकान्धनानि च; यदा वैरम्भेण — दृष्टं च श्रुतं च भुक्तं चाभुक्तं च सच्चासच्च सर्वं पश्यति । अथेमा दश दश नाड्यो भवन्ति, तासामेकैकस्य द्वासप्ततिर्द्वासप्ततिः शाखानाडीसहस्राणि भवन्ति । यस्मिन्नयमात्मा स्वपिति शब्दानां च करोति; अथ यद्द्वितीये सङ्कोशे स्वपिति तदेमं च लोकं परं च लोकं पश्यति, सर्वाञ्छब्दान्विजानाति । स सम्प्रसाद इत्याचक्षते । प्राणः शरीरं परिरक्षति । हरितस्य नीलस्य पीतस्य लोहितस्य श्वेतस्य नाड्यो रुधिरस्य पूर्णाः । अथ यथा केशः सहस्रधा भिन्नस्तथा हिता नाम नाड्यो भवन्ति । हृद्याकाशे परे कोशे दिव्योऽयमात्मा स्वपिति, यत्र सुप्तो न कञ्चन कामं कामयते न कञ्चन स्वप्नं पश्यति । न तत्र देवा न देवलोका न यज्ञा न माता न पिता न बन्धुर्न बान्धवो न स्तेनो न ब्रह्महा । तेजस्कायममृतं — सलिल एवेदं सलिलम् । वनं भूयस्तेनैव मार्गेण जाग्राय धावति सम्राडिति होवाच ॥ इति चतुर्थः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["hṛdayasya madhye lohitaṃ māṃsa-piṇḍaṃ, yasmiṃs tad daharaṃ puṇḍarīkaṃ kumudam ivānekadhā vikasitam | hṛdayasya daśa chidrāṇi bhavanti yeṣu prāṇāḥ pratiṣṭhitāḥ | sa yadā prāṇena saha saṃyujyate tadā paśyati nadyo nagarāṇi bahūni vividhāni ca; yadā vyānena — devāṃś ca ṛṣīṃś ca; yadāpānena — yakṣa-rākṣasa-gandharvān; yadā udānena — devalokān devān skandaṃ jayantaṃ ca; yadā samānena — devalokān dhanāni ca; yadā vairambheṇa — dṛṣṭaṃ ca śrutaṃ ca bhuktaṃ cābhuktaṃ ca sac cāsac ca sarvaṃ paśyati | atha imā daśa daśa nāḍyo bhavanti, tāsām ekaikasya dvā-saptatir dvā-saptatiḥ śākhā-nāḍī-sahasrāṇi bhavanti | yasminn ayam ātmā svapiti śabdānāṃ ca karoti; atha yad dvitīye saṅkośe svapiti tad emaṃ ca lokaṃ paraṃ ca lokaṃ paśyati, sarvāñ chabdān vijānāti | sa samprasāda ity ācakṣate | prāṇaḥ śarīraṃ parirakṣati | haritasya nīlasya pītasya lohitasya śvetasya nāḍyo rudhirasya pūrṇāḥ | atha yathā keśaḥ sahasradhā bhinnas tathā hitā nāma nāḍyo bhavanti | hṛdy-ākāśe pare kośe divyo 'yam ātmā svapiti, yatra supto na kañcana kāmaṃ kāmayate na kañcana svapnaṃ paśyati | na tatra devā na devalokā na yajñā na mātā na pitā na bandhur na bāndhavo na steno na brahmahā | tejaskāyam amṛtaṃ — salila evedaṃ salilam | vanaṃ bhūyas tenaiva mārgeṇa jāgrāya dhāvati samrāḍ iti hovāca ‖"],"words":[["hṛdayasya madhye lohitaṃ māṃsa-piṇḍam","loc. + nom.","\"in the middle of the chest, a red lump of flesh\""],["daharaṃ puṇḍarīkaṃ kumudam iva vikasitam","nom.","\"a small lotus, opened out like a water-lily\""],["daśa chidrāṇi … yeṣu prāṇāḥ pratiṣṭhitāḥ","nom.","\"ten apertures, in which the breaths are fixed\""],["prāṇena saha saṃyuktaḥ … paśyati","instr. + pres.","\"joined with (each) breath, he sees (its proper objects)\""],["vairambheṇa … sac ca asac ca sarvaṃ paśyati","instr.","\"with the vairambha (breath) he sees all — seen and unseen, eaten and uneaten, real and unreal\""],["daśa daśa nāḍyaḥ; ekaikasya dvā-saptatiḥ … sahasrāṇi","nom.","\"ten times ten channels; each with 72,000 branch-channels\""],["dvitīye saṅkośe svapiti","loc. + pres.","\"(when) he sleeps in the second sheath\" (dream)"],["sa samprasādaḥ iti ācakṣate","nom.","\"that (deep-sleep self) they call the 'serene one' (samprasāda)\""],["hitā nāma nāḍyaḥ … keśaḥ sahasradhā bhinnaḥ","nom.","\"the channels called hitā, (fine) as a hair split a thousandfold\""],["hṛdy-ākāśe pare kośe divyaḥ ayam ātmā svapiti","loc. + nom.","\"in the ether of the heart, the supreme sheath, this divine Self sleeps\""],["na kañcana kāmaṃ kāmayate / na svapnaṃ paśyati","pres.","\"desires no desire, sees no dream\" (deep sleep)"],["tejaskāyam amṛtam","acc.","\"the radiant-bodied, immortal (Self)\""],["salila eva idaṃ salilam","nom.","\"this (in deep sleep) is (pure) water — the 'ocean'\" (BĀU 4.3.32, salila eko draṣṭā)"],["tenaiva mārgeṇa jāgrāya dhāvati","instr. + dat.","\"by that very path he runs back to waking\""]],"lit":"\"In the middle of the chest is a red lump of flesh, in which is that small lotus, opened out like a water-lily. The heart has ten apertures, in which the breaths are fixed. When (the self) is joined with <em>prāṇa</em>, he sees many and varied rivers and cities; with <em>vyāna</em>, gods and seers; with <em>apāna</em>, Yakṣas, Rākṣasas, Gandharvas; with <em>udāna</em>, the god-worlds, the gods, Skanda and Jayanta; with <em>samāna</em>, the god-worlds and riches; with the <em>vairambha</em> breath he sees all — seen and unseen, eaten and uneaten, real and unreal. Now these are ten times ten channels, and each of them has seventy-two thousand branch-channels. In them this self sleeps and makes sounds. When he sleeps in the second sheath (dream), he sees both this world and the other, and cognizes all sounds; that (deep-sleep self) they call the 'serene one' (<em>samprasāda</em>). The breath guards the body. The channels are full of (humours) green, blue, yellow, red, white — and of blood. And, fine as a hair split a thousandfold, are the channels called <em>hitā</em>. In the ether of the heart, the supreme sheath, this divine Self sleeps, where, asleep, he desires no desire and sees no dream: there are no gods, no god-worlds, no sacrifices, no mother, no father, no kinsman, no relative, no thief, no Brahman-slayer. (He is) radiant-bodied, immortal — this is (pure) water, the (one) ocean. Then by that very path he runs back again to waking, said the Emperor (Self).\"","para":"The teaching of the <strong>heart and the three states</strong>, woven from Chāndogya 8 and Bṛhadāraṇyaka 2 &amp; 4. In the heart-lotus are ten apertures seating the breaths; in <strong>waking/dream</strong> the self, conjoined with each breath, perceives a corresponding visionary world (the <em>vairambha</em> breath granting omniscient vision of \"real and unreal\"). The <strong>nāḍī-network</strong> (100 principal, each with 72,000 branches; the hair-fine <em>hitā</em> channels) is the substrate of dream. In <strong>deep sleep</strong>, the Self rests in the <em>hṛd-ākāśa</em> (heart-ether), the \"supreme sheath,\" as the <em>samprasāda</em> (\"serene one\") — desireless, dreamless, beyond all relations and all moral categories (no father, no thief, no Brahman-slayer: the dissolution of duality named in BĀU 4.3.22). There it is the \"<strong>one ocean</strong>\" (<em>salila</em>, BĀU 4.3.32), pure radiant awareness — from which \"by the same path\" it returns to waking. The Self is thus identified as the constant witness across the three states.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","maps the passage to the <em>avasthā-traya</em> (waking/dream/sleep) analysis: the <em>samprasāda</em> of deep sleep is the <em>prājña</em>, the nearest empirical pointer to <em>turīya</em> (named in kh. 5, 9)"],["Paul Deussen","documents the dense Chāndogya/BĀU borrowing (the ten apertures, the 72,000 nāḍīs, the coloured humours, <em>salila</em>) and reads kh. 4 as Subāla's \"psychology of sleep.\""],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","takes the heart-cave Self as the <strong>antaryāmin</strong> Nārāyaṇa (kh. 6–7), the indweller who is the support of the sleeping jīva"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","uses the deep-sleep analysis as the practical bridge to meditation (the dreamless serenity as a foretaste of the Self)"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the breath-visions and <em>hitā</em>-nāḍīs closely"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 5","title":"The Inner Self Behind Every Organ (adhyātma · adhibhūta · adhidaivata)","sa":["चक्षुरध्यात्मं द्रष्टव्यमधिभूतमादित्यस्तत्राधिदैवतं नाडी तेषां निबन्धनम् । यश्चक्षुषि यो द्रष्टव्ये य आदित्ये यो नाड्यां यः प्राणे यो विज्ञाने य आनन्दे यो हृद्याकाशे य एतस्मिन्सर्वस्मिन्नन्तरे संचरति सोऽयमात्मा । तमात्मानमुपासीताजरममृतमभयमशोकमनन्तम् ॥","एष सर्वज्ञ एष सर्वेश्वर एष सर्वाधिपतिरेषोऽन्तर्यामी एष योनिः सर्वस्य । ... नान्तःप्रज्ञो न बहिःप्रज्ञो नोभयतःप्रज्ञो न प्रज्ञानघनो न प्रज्ञो नाप्रज्ञः । ... एकत्वं नास्ति द्वैतं कुतो, मर्त्यं नास्त्यमृतं कुतः । इत्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥"],"iast":["cakṣur adhyātmaṃ draṣṭavyam adhibhūtam ādityas tatrādhidaivataṃ nāḍī teṣāṃ nibandhanam | yaś cakṣuṣi yo draṣṭavye ya āditye yo nāḍyāṃ yaḥ prāṇe yo vijñāne ya ānande yo hṛdy-ākāśe ya etasmin sarvasminn antare saṃcarati so 'yam ātmā | tam ātmānam upāsītājaram amṛtam abhayam aśokam anantam ‖","eṣa sarvajña eṣa sarveśvara eṣa sarvādhipatir eṣo 'ntaryāmy eṣa yoniḥ sarvasya … nāntaḥprajño na bahiḥprajño nobhayataḥprajño na prajñānaghano na prajño nāprajñaḥ … ekatvaṃ nāsti dvaitaṃ kuto, martyaṃ nāsty amṛtaṃ kutaḥ … ity etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam ‖"],"words":[["cakṣuḥ adhyātmam","n. nom.","\"the eye is the 'pertaining-to-self' (the subjective organ)\""],["draṣṭavyam adhibhūtam","n. nom.","\"the visible is the 'pertaining-to-being' (the object)\""],["ādityaḥ tatra adhidaivatam","nom.","\"the sun there is the 'pertaining-to-deity' (the cosmic power)\""],["nāḍī teṣāṃ nibandhanam","nom. + gen.","\"the channel is their binding-link\""],["yaḥ cakṣuṣi … yo draṣṭavye … ya āditye … yo nāḍyāṃ","loc. (rel.)","\"he who (moves) in the eye, in the visible, in the sun, in the channel…\""],["yaḥ prāṇe yo vijñāne ya ānande yo hṛdy-ākāśe","loc.","\"…in the breath, in cognition, in bliss, in the heart-ether\""],["ya etasmin sarvasmin antare saṃcarati","loc. + pres.","\"who moves within this whole (series)\""],["saḥ ayam ātmā","nom.","\"that is this Self\""],["tam ātmānam upāsīta","acc. + opt.","\"that Self one should worship/meditate on\""],["ajaram amṛtam abhayam aśokam anantam","acc.","\"as ageless, immortal, fearless, sorrowless, endless\""]],"lit":"<strong>The fourteen substitutions</strong> (organ → object → presiding deity; the inner chain <em>channel → breath → cognition → bliss → heart-ether</em> is constant):<br>\"The eye is the subjective (organ), the visible the objective, the sun the presiding deity; the channel is their binding-link. He who moves within the eye, the visible, the sun, the channel, the breath, cognition, bliss, the heart-ether — who moves within this whole (series) — that is this Self. That Self one should worship as ageless, immortal, fearless, sorrowless, endless.\" … (so for all fourteen organs) … \"This is the omniscient, this the Lord of all, this the ruler of all, this the <strong>inner controller</strong> (<em>antaryāmin</em>), this the womb of all. … (He is) not inward-knowing, not outward-knowing, not both-knowing, not a mass of cognition, not knowing, not not-knowing. … There is oneness — whence duality? There is no mortal — whence the immortal? This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.\"","para":"A systematic <strong>triple correlation</strong> (<em>adhyātma</em> organ / <em>adhibhūta</em> object / <em>adhidaivata</em> cosmic deity) across all fourteen faculties, with the one Self threaded through each as the mover within the whole series — organ, object, deity, channel, breath, cognition, bliss, and the heart-ether. The meditative instruction is constant: <em>worship that Self</em> as ageless and fearless. The conclusion makes the Self the <strong>antaryāmin</strong> (\"inner controller\") and \"womb of all,\" and then — quoting <strong>Māṇḍūkya 7</strong> almost verbatim (<em>nāntaḥprajño…</em>) — places it beyond all cognitive states, asserting non-dual <em>ekatva</em> (\"there is oneness; whence duality?\").","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the litany as a graded <em>upāsanā</em> leading from the outer organ inward to the <em>turīya</em> Self (the Māṇḍūkya conclusion)"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","seizes on <em>eṣo 'ntaryāmī … yoniḥ sarvasya</em>: the indweller of every organ and deity is the personal Lord, the <em>śarīrin</em> of all; <em>ekatva</em> is the unity of the one Lord who is the Self of all selves, not the denial of real distinctions"],["Paul Deussen","notes the <em>adhyātma/adhibhūta/adhidaivata</em> scheme from BĀU/Gītā 8 and the Māṇḍūkya-7 quotation"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","gives the practical drill (trace each perception inward through the seven-fold series to the witness)"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the frame once and tabulates, as here"]],"notes":["<em>This khaṇḍa is a <strong>litany</strong>: a single frame is repeated for each of the fourteen organs. The frame is glossed once below; the full set of substitutions follows as a table.</em>","<strong>The repeated frame (taking the eye as the model):</strong>","<table><tr><td>#</td><td>adhyātma (organ)</td><td>adhibhūta (object)</td><td>adhidaivata (deity)</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>cakṣus — eye</td><td>draṣṭavya — the visible</td><td>Āditya (sun)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>śrotra — ear</td><td>śrotavya — the audible</td><td>Diśaḥ (quarters)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>nāsā — nose</td><td>ghrātavya — the smellable</td><td>Pṛthivī (earth)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>jihvā — tongue</td><td>rasayitavya — the tastable</td><td>Varuṇa <em>(e-text wrongly repeats the skin/touch clause here)</em></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>tvak — skin</td><td>sparśayitavya — the touchable</td><td>Vāyu</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>manas — mind</td><td>mantavya — the thinkable</td><td>Candra (moon)</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>buddhi — intellect</td><td>boddhavya — the knowable</td><td>Brahmā</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>ahaṃkāra — ego</td><td>ahaṃkartavya — the I-makeable</td><td>Rudra</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>citta — thought</td><td>cetayitavya — the conceivable</td><td>Kṣetrajña</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>vāc — speech</td><td>vaktavya — the speakable</td><td>Agni</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>hastau — hands</td><td>ādātavya — the graspable</td><td>Indra</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>pādau — feet</td><td>gantavya — the walkable</td><td>Viṣṇu</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>pāyu — anus</td><td>visarjayitavya — the excretable</td><td>Mṛtyu</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>upastha — genitals</td><td>ānandayitavya — the pleasurable</td><td>Prajāpati</td></tr></table>","<strong>The concluding declaration:</strong>"]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 6","title":"All Is Nārāyaṇa","sa":["नैवेह किंचनाग्र आसीदमूलमनाधारम् । इमाः प्रजाः प्रजायन्ते दिव्यो देव एको नारायणः । चक्षुश्च द्रष्टव्यं च नारायणः, श्रोत्रं च श्रोतव्यं च नारायणः, ... मनश्च मन्तव्यं च नारायणः, बुद्धिश्च बोद्धव्यं च नारायणः, अहंकारश्चाहंकर्तव्यं च नारायणः, चित्तं च चेतयितव्यं च नारायणः, वाक् च वक्तव्यं च नारायणः, ... नारायणो धाता विधाता कर्ता विकर्ता दिव्यो देव एको नारायणः । आदित्या रुद्रा मरुतो वसवोऽश्विनावृचो यजूंषि सामानि मन्त्रोऽग्निराज्याहुतिर्नारायणः । ... नारायणो माता पिता भ्राता निवासः शरणं सुहृद्गतिर्नारायणः । ... सर्वं नारायणः ॥ पुरुष एवेदं सर्वं यद्भूतं यच्च भव्यम् । उतामृतत्वस्येशानो यदन्नेनातिरोहति ॥ तद्विष्णोः परमं पदं सदा पश्यन्ति सूरयः । दिवीव चक्षुराततम् ॥ तद्विप्रासो विपन्यवो जागृवांसः समिन्धते । विष्णोर्यत्परमं पदम् ॥ तदेतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥"],"iast":["naiveha kiṃcanāgra āsīd amūlam anādhāram | imāḥ prajāḥ prajāyante divyo deva eko nārāyaṇaḥ | cakṣuś ca draṣṭavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ, śrotraṃ ca śrotavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ, … manaś ca mantavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ, buddhiś ca boddhavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ, ahaṃkāraś cāhaṃkartavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ, cittaṃ ca cetayitavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ, vāk ca vaktavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ … nārāyaṇo dhātā vidhātā kartā vikartā divyo deva eko nārāyaṇaḥ | ādityā rudrā maruto vasavo 'śvināv ṛco yajūṃṣi sāmāni mantro 'gnir ājyāhutir nārāyaṇaḥ … nārāyaṇo mātā pitā bhrātā nivāsaḥ śaraṇaṃ suhṛd gatir nārāyaṇaḥ … sarvaṃ nārāyaṇaḥ ‖ puruṣa evedaṃ sarvaṃ yad bhūtaṃ yac ca bhavyam | utāmṛtatvasyeśāno yad annenātirohati ‖ tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padaṃ sadā paśyanti sūrayaḥ | divīva cakṣur ātatam ‖ tad viprāso vipanyavo jāgṛvāṃsaḥ samindhate | viṣṇor yat paramaṃ padam ‖"],"words":[["na eva iha kiṃcana agre āsīt","\"nothing whatever was here in the beginning\"","\"in the beginning nothing whatever existed (separately)\""],["amūlam anādhāram","n. acc.","\"without root, without support\""],["imāḥ prajāḥ prajāyante","nom. + pres.","\"(yet) these creatures are born\""],["divyaḥ devaḥ ekaḥ nārāyaṇaḥ","nom.","\"the one divine God, Nārāyaṇa\""],["cakṣuś ca draṣṭavyaṃ ca nārāyaṇaḥ","nom.","\"both the eye and the visible are Nārāyaṇa\" (so for every organ + object)"],["dhātā vidhātā kartā vikartā","nom.","\"the ordainer, disposer, doer, undoer\""],["nārāyaṇaḥ mātā pitā bhrātā nivāsaḥ śaraṇaṃ suhṛd gatiḥ","nom.","\"Nārāyaṇa is mother, father, brother, dwelling, refuge, friend, goal\""],["puruṣa evedaṃ sarvam…","(ṚV 10.90.2)","\"the Person alone is all this that was and will be\""],["utāmṛtatvasya īśānaḥ","nom.","\"lord, too, of immortality\""],["tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam","(ṚV 1.22.20)","\"that supreme abode of Viṣṇu\""],["sadā paśyanti sūrayaḥ","pres.","\"the wise ever behold (it)\""],["divīva cakṣur ātatam","\"like an eye fixed in heaven\"","\"spread out like an eye in the sky\""],["viprāsaḥ vipanyavaḥ jāgṛvāṃsaḥ samindhate","(ṚV 1.22.21)","\"the wakeful, praising sages kindle it\""]],"lit":"\"In the beginning nothing whatever existed here, rootless and supportless; (yet) these creatures are born — the one divine God, Nārāyaṇa. The eye and the visible are Nārāyaṇa; the ear and the audible are Nārāyaṇa; … mind and the thinkable, intellect and the knowable, ego and the I-makeable, thought and the conceivable, speech and the speakable — (all are) Nārāyaṇa; … Nārāyaṇa is the ordainer, disposer, doer, undoer — the one divine God, Nārāyaṇa. The Ādityas, Rudras, Maruts, Vasus, the two Aśvins, the Ṛk-, Yajus-, Sāman-verses, the sacred formula, the fire, the ghee-oblation — (all are) Nārāyaṇa. … Nārāyaṇa is mother, father, brother, dwelling, refuge, friend, and goal. … All is Nārāyaṇa. 'The Person alone is all this that was and will be; He is the lord, too, of immortality, (the Person) who grows by food.' 'That supreme abode of Viṣṇu the wise ever behold, spread out like an eye in the sky.' 'The wakeful, praising sages kindle that supreme abode of Viṣṇu.' This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's <strong>devotional summit</strong> and the keystone of its Vaiṣṇava reading. From the rootless, supportless \"nothing\" (the <em>avyākṛta</em>) the one God <strong>Nārāyaṇa</strong> brings forth all creatures and <em>is</em> all things — every organ and its object, every cosmic power, every Veda and rite, and every human relation (mother, father, refuge, goal). The prose culminates in three of the most famous Ṛgvedic theophany-verses: the <strong>Puruṣa-sūkta</strong>'s \"the Person is all this\" (10.90.2) and the two <strong>Viṣṇu</strong> verses on \"the supreme abode of Viṣṇu\" (1.22.20–21) which the <em>sūrayaḥ</em> (the wise/the seers) eternally behold.","coms":[["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","treats this as the decisive Subāla passage: <em>eko nārāyaṇaḥ</em> names Brahman as the personal Lord Viṣṇu-Nārāyaṇa, and \"all is Nārāyaṇa\" states the <em>aprthak-siddhi</em> (inseparable dependence) of all things on Him as His body — the very basis of Viśiṣṭādvaita; <em>tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam</em> is the goal, <em>Vaikuṇṭha</em>/the Lord's own nature"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads \"all is Nārāyaṇa\" as the Advaitic <em>sarvaṃ brahma</em> with \"Nārāyaṇa\" (= <em>nāra</em> + <em>ayana</em>, the ground of all) as a name of the attributeless Self"],["Paul Deussen","notes the wholesale incorporation of the Nārāyaṇa-/Puruṣa-/Viṣṇu-sūkta material and dates this stratum to the rise of Vaiṣṇava theism"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","reads it as the warrant for <em>bhakti</em> to the personal God as a path identical in goal with <em>jñāna</em>"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the litany and the three ṛcas"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 7","title":"The Antaryāmin: Whose Body Is the Universe","sa":["अन्तःशरीरे निहितो गुहायामज एको नित्यः — यस्य पृथिवी शरीरं यः पृथिवीमन्तरे संचरन्यं पृथिवी न वेद ॥","स एष सर्वभूतान्तरात्मापहतपाप्मा दिव्यो देव एको नारायणः । एतां विद्यामपान्तरतमाय ददौ, अपान्तरतमो ब्रह्मणे ददौ, ब्रह्मा घोराङ्गिरसे ददौ, घोराङ्गिरा रैक्वाय ददौ, रैक्वो रामाय ददौ, रामः सर्वेभ्यो भूतेभ्यो ददौ — इत्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥"],"iast":["antaḥśarīre nihito guhāyām aja eko nityaḥ — yasya pṛthivī śarīraṃ yaḥ pṛthivīm antare saṃcaran yaṃ pṛthivī na veda ‖","sa eṣa sarva-bhūtāntarātmāpahata-pāpmā divyo deva eko nārāyaṇaḥ | etāṃ vidyām apāntaratamāya dadau, apāntaratamo brahmaṇe dadau, brahmā ghorāṅgirase dadau, ghorāṅgirā raikvāya dadau, raikvo rāmāya dadau, rāmaḥ sarvebhyo bhūtebhyo dadau — ity etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam ‖"],"words":[["antaḥ-śarīre nihitaḥ guhāyām","loc. + nom.","\"lodged within the body, in the cave (of the heart)\""],["ajaḥ ekaḥ nityaḥ","nom.","\"unborn, one, eternal\""],["yasya pṛthivī śarīram","gen. + nom.","\"whose body is the earth\""],["yaḥ pṛthivīm antare saṃcaran","nom. + part.","\"who, moving within the earth,\""],["yaṃ pṛthivī na veda","acc. + pres.","\"whom the earth knows not\""]],"lit":"<strong>The twelve \"bodies\"</strong> (the same frame, <em>yasya X śarīraṃ … yaṃ X na veda</em>): <strong>pṛthivī</strong> (earth) · <strong>āpas</strong> (water) · <strong>tejas</strong> (fire) · <strong>vāyu</strong> (air) · <strong>ākāśa</strong> (ether) · <strong>manas</strong> (mind) · <strong>buddhi</strong> (intellect) · <strong>ahaṃkāra</strong> (ego) · <strong>citta</strong> (thought) · <strong>avyakta</strong> (the unmanifest) · <strong>akṣara</strong> (the imperishable) · <strong>mṛtyu</strong> (death).<br>\"Lodged within the body, in the cave (of the heart), is the unborn, one, eternal (Self) — whose body is the earth, who moves within the earth, whom the earth knows not. (So too) whose body is the water… fire… air… ether… mind… intellect… ego… thought… the unmanifest… the imperishable… death — who moves within each, whom each knows not. He, this <strong>Self within all beings</strong>, sinless, is the one divine God, Nārāyaṇa. This knowledge He gave to Apāntaratamas; Apāntaratamas gave it to Brahmā; Brahmā to Ghorāṅgiras; Ghorāṅgiras to Raikva; Raikva to Rāma; Rāma gave it to all beings. This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.\"","para":"The <strong>antaryāmin</strong> doctrine — the inner ruler \"whose body is X, who dwells within X, whom X knows not\" — taken straight from <strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.7</strong> (the Yājñavalkya–Uddālaka dialogue) but <strong>extended</strong> beyond the cosmic elements to the entire psychic apparatus (mind, intellect, ego, thought) and the metaphysical principles (the unmanifest, the imperishable, even death). The unborn Self in the heart-cave is the controller of all twenty-... twelve \"bodies,\" yet transcends them all (each \"knows Him not\"). This indweller is identified as <strong>Nārāyaṇa</strong>, \"the Self of all beings,\" and the teaching is given a <strong>transmission-lineage</strong> (Apāntaratamas → Brahmā → Ghorāṅgiras → Raikva → Rāma → all beings), introducing the pupil Raikva who questions in khaṇḍas 9–11, 15.","coms":[["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","treats this as <em>the</em> foundational antaryāmin text alongside BĀU 3.7: the world and the soul are the"],["body","(<em>śarīra</em>) of the Lord, who is their <strong>inner Self</strong> (<em>śarīrin</em>) — present in all, controlling all, yet untouched and unknown by them; this <em>aprthak-siddha-viśeṣaṇa</em> relation is the core of Viśiṣṭādvaita, and \"<em>eko nārāyaṇaḥ</em>\" names that inner ruler as Viṣṇu"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the same as the one Self appearing as the inner witness of all <em>upādhis</em>, \"whom they know not\" because It is their very knower"],["Paul Deussen","marks the BĀU 3.7 borrowing and the Sāṃkhya extension (avyakta, akṣara)"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","uses the litany as a meditation on the indweller behind every layer"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the frame and tabulates the twelve"]],"notes":["<em>A second <strong>litany</strong> — the celebrated antaryāmin (inner-controller) frame, parallel to Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.7, repeated across twelve \"bodies.\"</em>","<strong>The repeated frame:</strong>","<strong>The conclusion and the lineage:</strong>"]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 8","title":"The Pure Self Hidden in the Impure Body","sa":["अन्तःशरीरे निहितो गुहायां शुद्धः सोऽयमात्मा । सर्वस्य मेदोमांसक्लेदावकीर्णे शरीरमध्येऽत्यन्तोपहते चित्रभित्तिप्रतीकाशे गन्धर्वनगरोपमे कदलीगर्भवन्निःसारे जलबुद्बुदवच्चञ्चले निःसृतमात्मानम् — अचिन्त्यरूपं दिव्यं देवमसङ्गं शुद्धं तेजस्कायमरूपं सर्वेश्वरमचिन्त्यमशरीरं निहितं गुहायाममृतं विभ्राजमानमानन्दम् — तं पश्यन्ति विद्वांसः, तेन लये न पश्यन्ति ॥ इति अष्टमः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["antaḥśarīre nihito guhāyāṃ śuddhaḥ so 'yam ātmā | sarvasya medo-māṃsa-kledāvakīrṇe śarīra-madhye 'tyantopahate citra-bhitti-pratīkāśe gandharva-nagaropame kadalī-garbhavan niḥsāre jala-budbudavac cañcale niḥsṛtam ātmānam — acintya-rūpaṃ divyaṃ devam asaṅgaṃ śuddhaṃ tejaskāyam arūpaṃ sarveśvaram acintyam aśarīraṃ nihitaṃ guhāyām amṛtaṃ vibhrājamānam ānandam — taṃ paśyanti vidvāṃsaḥ, tena laye na paśyanti ‖"],"words":[["antaḥ-śarīre nihitaḥ guhāyāṃ śuddhaḥ ayam ātmā","loc. + nom.","\"lodged within the body, in the cave, (is) this pure Self\""],["medas-māṃsa-kleda-avakīrṇe","loc.","\"(in the body) strewn with fat, flesh, and ooze\""],["śarīra-madhye atyanta-upahate","loc.","\"in the midst of the utterly foul body\""],["citra-bhitti-pratīkāśe","loc.","\"resembling a painted wall\""],["gandharva-nagara-upame","loc.","\"like a city of Gandharvas (a mirage-city)\""],["kadalī-garbhavat niḥsāre","loc.","\"pithless as a plantain-trunk's core\""],["jala-budbudavat cañcale","loc.","\"unsteady as a water-bubble\""],["niḥsṛtam ātmānam","acc.","\"the Self that has come forth (stands apart)\""],["acintya-rūpaṃ … asaṅgaṃ śuddhaṃ tejaskāyam arūpam","acc.","\"of unthinkable form, unattached, pure, radiant-bodied, formless\""],["sarveśvaram acintyam aśarīram","acc.","\"the Lord of all, unthinkable, bodiless\""],["amṛtaṃ vibhrājamānam ānandam","acc.","\"immortal, shining, blissful\""],["taṃ paśyanti vidvāṃsaḥ","acc. + pres.","\"the wise behold Him\""],["tena laye na paśyanti","instr. + pres.","\"through that (their) absorption, they see no (more diversity)\" / \"owing to (their own) dissolution (the ignorant) see Him not\""]],"lit":"\"Lodged within the body, in the cave, is this pure Self. In the midst of the utterly foul body — strewn with fat, flesh, and ooze, resembling a painted wall, like a mirage-city of Gandharvas, pithless as a plantain-trunk, unsteady as a water-bubble — (the wise behold) the Self that has come forth (and stands apart): of unthinkable form, divine, the shining God, unattached, pure, radiant-bodied, formless, the Lord of all, unthinkable, bodiless, lodged in the cave, immortal, resplendent, blissful. Him the wise behold; through that (absorption) they see no (diversity).\"","para":"The <strong>discrimination of the pure Self from the impure body</strong> — using the very chain of <em>māyā</em>-similes found in Maitri 4.2 (painted wall, Gandharva-city/mirage, pithless plantain, water-bubble). Amid the foul, fragile, illusory body dwells the Self — <em>acintya</em> (unthinkable), <em>asaṅga</em> (unattached), <em>tejaskāya</em> (radiant), <em>sarveśvara</em> (Lord of all), <em>ānanda</em> (bliss). This the <em>vidvān</em> (the wise) \"sees\"; and in that vision/absorption (<em>laya</em>) the seer no longer sees the manifold (or: the unwise, being themselves dissolved in the body, do not see Him) — the final clause is deliberately double-edged.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the similes as establishing <em>deha-vailakṣaṇya</em> (the Self's utter difference from the body); <em>tena laye na paśyanti</em> = \"in the absorption (of knowledge) they see no second.\""],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","takes <em>sarveśvaram… asaṅgam</em> as the Lord dwelling in the heart, untouched by the body that is His instrument; the wise \"see Him\" by <em>bhakti</em>-illumined knowledge"],["Paul Deussen","notes the shared simile-stock with Maitri (common late-Upaniṣadic <em>vairāgya</em> imagery)"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","makes this the core <em>dehātma-buddhi-nivṛtti</em> (removal of body-identification) practice"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders closely, taking the last clause as \"the ignorant, through delusion, see Him not.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 9","title":"\"In What Do All Things Set?\": The Reabsorption, and How the Self Is Known","sa":["अथ हैनं रैक्वः पप्रच्छ — भगवन्कस्मिन्सर्वेऽस्तं गच्छन्तीति । तस्मै स होवाच — चक्षुरेवाप्येति यश्चक्षुरेवास्तमेति, द्रष्टव्यमेवाप्येति यो द्रष्टव्यमेवास्तमेति, आदित्यमेवाप्येति ..., विराजम् ..., प्राणम् ..., विज्ञानम् ..., आनन्दम् ..., तुरीयमेवाप्येति यस्तुरीयमेवास्तमेति, तदमृतमभयमशोकमनन्तनिर्बीजमेवाप्येतीति होवाच ॥","य एवं निर्बीजं वेद निर्बीज एव स भवति, न जायते न म्रियते न मुह्यते न भिद्यते न दह्यते न छिद्यते न कम्पते न कुप्यते — सर्वदहनोऽयमात्मेत्याचक्षते । नैवमात्मा प्रवचनशतेनापि लभ्यते, न बहुश्रुतेन, न बुद्धिज्ञानाश्रितेन, न मेधया, न वेदैर्न यज्ञैर्न तपोभिरुग्रैर्न सांख्यैर्न योगैर्नाश्रमैर्नान्यैरात्मानमुपलभन्ते । ... शान्तो दान्त उपरतस्तितिक्षुः समाहितो भूत्वात्मन्येवात्मानं पश्यति, सर्वस्यात्मा भवति य एवं वेद ॥ इति नवमः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ raikvaḥ papraccha — bhagavan kasmin sarve 'staṃ gacchantīti | tasmai sa hovāca — cakṣur evāpyeti yaś cakṣur evāstam eti, draṣṭavyam evāpyeti yo draṣṭavyam evāstam eti, ādityam evāpyeti …, virājam …, prāṇam …, vijñānam …, ānandam …, turīyam evāpyeti yas turīyam evāstam eti, tad amṛtam abhayam aśokam ananta-nirbījam evāpyetīti hovāca ‖","ya evaṃ nirbījaṃ veda nirbīja eva sa bhavati, na jāyate na mriyate na muhyate na bhidyate na dahyate na chidyate na kampate na kupyate — sarva-dahano 'yam ātmety ācakṣate | naivam ātmā pravacana-śatenāpi labhyate, na bahu-śrutena, na buddhi-jñānāśritena, na medhayā, na vedair na yajñair na tapobhir ugrair na sāṃkhyair na yogair nāśramair nānyair ātmānam upalabhante | … śānto dānta uparatas titikṣuḥ samāhito bhūtvātmany evātmānaṃ paśyati, sarvasyātmā bhavati ya evaṃ veda ‖"],"words":[["atha ha enaṃ raikvaḥ papraccha","pf.","\"then Raikva asked him\""],["bhagavan kasmin sarve astaṃ gacchanti","loc. + pres.","\"Lord, in what do all things set (come to rest)?\""],["cakṣuḥ eva apyeti","pres. (api-eti)","\"the eye merges (into its ground)\""],["yaḥ cakṣuḥ eva astam eti","pres.","\"(in) that into which the eye sets\""],["ādityam … virājam … prāṇam … vijñānam … ānandam … turīyam","acc.","\"(it sets into) the sun… Virāj… breath… cognition… bliss… the Fourth\""],["tad amṛtam abhayam aśokam ananta-nirbījam evāpyeti","acc.","\"it merges into that immortal, fearless, sorrowless, endless, seedless (Reality)\""]],"lit":"<strong>The fourteen reabsorption-series</strong> (each: organ → object → deity → nāḍī → wind → <em>vijñāna</em> → … → <em>nirbīja</em>). The eye-series and the final (citta) series carry the full <em>vijñāna → ānanda → turīya → nirbīja</em> ascent; the rest run organ → object → deity → nāḍī → wind → <em>vijñāna</em> → <em>nirbīja</em>:<br>\"Then Raikva asked him: 'Lord, in what do all things come to rest?' He answered: 'The eye merges into that into which the eye sets — into the visible, the sun, Virāj, the breath, cognition, bliss, the Fourth; it merges into that immortal, fearless, sorrowless, endless, <strong>seedless</strong> (Reality).'\" … (so for all fourteen) … \"He who thus knows the Seedless becomes seedless indeed: he is not born, dies not, is not deluded, not split, not burnt, not cut, trembles not, is not angered — 'this Self is the burner of all,' they say. Not by a hundred expositions is this Self attained, nor by much learning, nor by reliance on intellect-knowledge, nor by genius, nor by the Vedas, nor by sacrifices, nor by fierce austerities, nor by Sāṃkhya, nor by Yoga, nor by the (other) life-stages, nor by anything else do (people) attain the Self. … Having become tranquil, controlled, withdrawn, forbearing, and collected, one sees the Self in the very self, and becomes the Self of all — he who knows thus.\"","para":"Raikva's question — <em>kasmin sarve 'staṃ gacchanti</em> (\"in what do all things set?\") — is answered by a grand <strong>reverse meditation</strong>: each faculty, with its object, presiding deity, channel, and vital current, \"sets\" into <em>vijñāna</em> (cognition) and finally into the <strong>nirbīja</strong> (\"seedless\") Reality — immortal, fearless, sorrowless, endless. To know this Seedless is to <em>become</em> seedless: unborn, undying, \"the burner of all.\" The conclusion then states, almost verbatim from <strong>Kaṭha 1.2.23</strong>, that the Self is <strong>not won</strong> by exposition, learning, intellect, Vedas, sacrifices, austerities, Sāṃkhya, Yoga, or āśrama — only the <strong>tranquil, controlled, withdrawn, forbearing, collected</strong> seeker (the <em>śānto dānto…</em> of <strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.23</strong>) \"sees the Self in the self and becomes the Self of all.\"","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","treats <em>nirbīja</em> as liberation (the \"seed\" of avidyā/karma burnt away) and reads the <em>naivam ātmā…labhyate</em> passage as the supremacy of <em>jñāna</em>-with-<em>sādhana-catuṣṭaya</em> over all mere means"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","reads \"becomes the Self of all\" not as identity-without-distinction but as the released soul's likeness to and communion with the all-pervading Lord; the means-list denies <em>human</em> effort as sufficient — the Self is won by His grace (prasāda) and <em>bhakti</em>"],["Paul Deussen","notes the Kaṭha 1.2.23 and BĀU 4.4.23 verbatim borrowings"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","centres the <em>śānto dānto</em> qualities as the practical heart of the path"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the litany and conclusion"]],"notes":["<em>The pupil <strong>Raikva</strong> opens the first of four dialogues. The answer is a <strong>litany</strong> of reabsorption, repeated across the fourteen faculties; the frame is glossed once, the substitutions tabulated, and the doctrinally crucial conclusion given in full.</em>","<table><tr><td>organ</td><td>object</td><td>deity</td><td>nāḍī</td><td>wind/current</td></tr><tr><td>cakṣus</td><td>draṣṭavya</td><td>āditya</td><td>(virāj)</td><td>prāṇa → vijñāna → ānanda → turīya</td></tr><tr><td>śrotra</td><td>śrotavya</td><td>diś</td><td>sudarśanā</td><td>apāna</td></tr><tr><td>nāsā</td><td>ghrātavya</td><td>pṛthivī</td><td>jitā</td><td>vyāna</td></tr><tr><td>jihvā</td><td>rasayitavya</td><td>varuṇa</td><td>saumyā</td><td>udāna</td></tr><tr><td>tvac</td><td>sparśayitavya</td><td>vāyu</td><td>moghā</td><td>samāna</td></tr><tr><td>vāc</td><td>vaktavya</td><td>agni</td><td>kumārā</td><td>vairambha</td></tr><tr><td>hasta</td><td>ādātavya</td><td>indra</td><td>amṛtā</td><td>mukhya</td></tr><tr><td>pāda</td><td>gantavya</td><td>viṣṇu</td><td>satyā</td><td>antaryāma</td></tr><tr><td>pāyu</td><td>visarjayitavya</td><td>mṛtyu</td><td>madhyamā</td><td>prabhañjana</td></tr><tr><td>upastha</td><td>ānandayitavya</td><td>prajāpati</td><td>nāsīrā</td><td>kumāra</td></tr><tr><td>manas</td><td>mantavya</td><td>candra</td><td>śiśu</td><td>śyena</td></tr><tr><td>buddhi</td><td>boddhavya</td><td>brahmā</td><td>sūryā</td><td>kṛṣṇa</td></tr><tr><td>ahaṃkāra</td><td>ahaṃkartavya</td><td>rudra</td><td>asurā</td><td>śveta</td></tr><tr><td>citta</td><td>cetayitavya</td><td>kṣetrajña</td><td>bhāsvatī</td><td>nāga → vijñāna → ānanda → turīya</td></tr></table>","<strong>The conclusion (in full):</strong>"]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 10","title":"\"In What Are All Things Established?\": The Worlds Within the Self","sa":["अथ हैनं रैक्वः पप्रच्छ — भगवन्कस्मिन्सर्वे सम्प्रतिष्ठिता भवन्तीति । रसातललोकेष्विति होवाच । कस्मिन्रसातललोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । भूर्लोकेष्विति होवाच । ... कस्मिन्ब्रह्मलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति — सर्वलोका आत्मनि ब्रह्मणि मणय इवौताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । स होवाच — एवमेतान्लोकानात्मनि प्रतिष्ठितान्वेद, आत्मैव स भवतीत्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥ इति दशमः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ raikvaḥ papraccha — bhagavan kasmin sarve sampratiṣṭhitā bhavantīti | rasātala-lokeṣv iti hovāca | kasmin rasātala-lokā otāś ca protāś ceti | bhūr-lokeṣv iti hovāca | … kasmin brahma-lokā otāś ca protāś ceti — sarva-lokā ātmani brahmaṇi maṇaya ivautāś ca protāś ceti | sa hovāca — evam etān lokān ātmani pratiṣṭhitān veda, ātmaiva sa bhavatīty etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam ‖"],"words":[["kasmin sarve sampratiṣṭhitāḥ bhavanti","loc. + pres.","\"in what are all things established?\""],["rasātala-lokeṣu iti","loc.","\"in the worlds of the nether-region\" (so he said)"],["kasmin … otāḥ ca protāḥ ca","nom.","\"in what are they 'warp and woof' (woven through)?\""],["bhūr-…bhuvar-…suvar-…mahar-…janas-…tapas-…satya-…prajāpati-…brahma-lokeṣu","loc.","\"(successively) in the earth-, mid-, heaven-, mahar-, janas-, tapas-, satya-, Prajāpati-, Brahmā-worlds\""],["sarva-lokāḥ ātmani brahmaṇi","loc.","\"all the worlds (are woven) in the Self, in Brahman\""],["maṇayaḥ iva otāḥ ca protāḥ ca","nom.","\"warp-and-woof like (strung) gems\""],["etān lokān ātmani pratiṣṭhitān veda","acc. + pres.","\"knows these worlds as established in the Self\""],["ātmā eva saḥ bhavati","nom.","\"he becomes the Self itself\""]],"lit":"\"Then Raikva asked him: 'Lord, in what are all things established?' 'In the nether-worlds,' he said. 'In what are the nether-worlds woven, warp and woof?' 'In the earth-worlds,' he said — and so through the mid-world, heaven-world, mahar-, janas-, tapas-, satya-, Prajāpati-, and Brahmā-worlds. 'In what are the Brahmā-worlds woven?' — 'All the worlds are woven, warp and woof, in the Self, in Brahman, like (strung) gems.' He said: 'He who thus knows these worlds as established in the Self becomes the Self itself.' This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.\"","para":"A <strong>regress of grounds</strong> (modelled on Gārgī's questioning in BĀU 3.6): each cosmic level is \"woven\" (<em>ota-prota</em>, warp-and-woof) into the one above, the whole ladder of worlds — from the nether regions up through the seven <em>vyāhṛti</em>-worlds to the Brahmā-world — finally woven <strong>into the Self/Brahman</strong>, \"like gems (strung on a thread).\" The regress halts not in another world but in the Self, which is the ground of all grounds; and to <strong>know</strong> the worlds as established in the Self is to <strong>become</strong> that Self.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the <em>ota-prota</em> regress as resolving all <em>adhāra</em> (supports) into the one <em>adhiṣṭhāna</em> (substrate), the Self"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","takes the gems-on-a-thread image as a perfect figure of <em>śarīra-śarīrin</em>: the worlds are real gems strung on the Lord who is their inner thread (sūtrātman) — distinct, yet inseparably dependent"],["Paul Deussen","notes the BĀU 3.6 (Gārgī) model"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","render the worlds-in-the-Self as the cosmological complement to the antaryāmin (kh. 7): God within all (7) and all within God (10)"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 11","title":"The Process of Dying: Breaking Through the Sheaths","sa":["अथ हैनं रैक्वः पप्रच्छ — भगवन्योऽयं विज्ञानघन उत्क्रामन्स केन कतरद्वा स्थानमुत्सृज्यापक्रामतीति । तस्मै स होवाच — हृदयस्य मध्ये लोहितं मांसपिण्डं यस्मिंस्तद्दहरं पुण्डरीकं कुमुदमिवानेकधा विकसितम्; तस्य मध्ये समुद्रः, समुद्रस्य मध्ये कोशः, तस्मिन्नाड्यश्चतस्रो भवन्ति — रमा अरमा इच्छा अपुनर्भवेति । तत्र रमा पुण्येन पुण्यं लोकं नयति, अरमा पापेन पापम्, इच्छया यत्स्मरति तदभिसम्पद्यते, अपुनर्भवया कोशं भिनत्ति; कोशं भित्त्वा शीर्षकपालं भिनत्ति, ... पृथिवीं भित्त्वापो भिनत्ति, ... आकाशं भित्त्वा मनो भिनत्ति, ... महान्तं भित्त्वाव्यक्तं भिनत्ति, अव्यक्तं भित्त्वाक्षरं भिनत्ति, अक्षरं भित्त्वा मृत्युं भिनत्ति — मृत्युर्वै परे देव एकीभवति । परस्तान्न सन्नासन्न सदसदित्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥ इत्येकादशः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ raikvaḥ papraccha — bhagavan yo 'yaṃ vijñāna-ghana utkrāman sa kena kataradvā sthānam utsṛjyāpakrāmatīti | tasmai sa hovāca — hṛdayasya madhye lohitaṃ māṃsa-piṇḍaṃ yasmiṃs tad daharaṃ puṇḍarīkaṃ kumudam ivānekadhā vikasitam; tasya madhye samudraḥ, samudrasya madhye kośaḥ, tasmin nāḍyaś catasro bhavanti — ramā aramā icchā apunarbhaveti | tatra ramā puṇyena puṇyaṃ lokaṃ nayati, aramā pāpena pāpam, icchayā yat smarati tad abhisampadyate, apunarbhavayā kośaṃ bhinatti; kośaṃ bhittvā śīrṣa-kapālaṃ bhinatti, … pṛthivīṃ bhittvāpo bhinatti, … ākāśaṃ bhittvā mano bhinatti, … mahāntaṃ bhittvāvyaktaṃ bhinatti, avyaktaṃ bhittvākṣaraṃ bhinatti, akṣaraṃ bhittvā mṛtyuṃ bhinatti — mṛtyur vai pare deva ekī-bhavati | parastān na sann āsan na sad-asad ity etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam ‖"],"words":[["yaḥ ayaṃ vijñāna-ghanaḥ utkrāman","nom. + part.","\"this mass-of-cognition (the soul), departing (at death)\""],["sa kena kataradvā sthānam utsṛjya apakrāmati","instr. + acc. + abs.","\"by what, and leaving which place, does it depart?\""],["hṛdayasya madhye … daharaṃ puṇḍarīkam","loc.","\"in the heart's middle … the small lotus\""],["tasya madhye samudraḥ, samudrasya madhye kośaḥ","loc.","\"in its centre an ocean; in the ocean's centre a sheath\""],["tasmin nāḍyaḥ catasraḥ — ramā, aramā, icchā, apunarbhavā","nom.","\"in it are four channels — Ramā, Aramā, Icchā, Apunarbhavā\""],["ramā puṇyena puṇyaṃ lokaṃ nayati","instr. + acc.","\"Ramā (the 'delightful') by merit leads to a meritorious world\""],["aramā pāpena pāpam","instr.","\"Aramā by demerit (leads) to an evil (world)\""],["icchayā yat smarati tad abhisampadyate","instr. + pres.","\"by Icchā ('desire') one attains whatever one remembers (at death)\""],["apunarbhavayā kośaṃ bhinatti","instr. + pres.","\"by Apunarbhavā ('no-rebirth') one pierces the sheath\""],["kośaṃ bhittvā śīrṣa-kapālaṃ bhinatti","abs. + pres.","\"having pierced the sheath, one pierces the skull\""],["pṛthivīṃ … āpaḥ … tejaḥ … vāyum … ākāśaṃ … mano … (mahat) … avyaktaṃ … akṣaraṃ … mṛtyuṃ bhinatti","acc. chain","\"pierces (in turn) earth, water, fire, air, ether, mind, the mahat, the unmanifest, the imperishable, death\""],["mṛtyuḥ vai pare deve ekī-bhavati","loc. + pres.","\"death becomes one with the supreme God\""]],"lit":"\"Then Raikva asked him: 'Lord, this mass-of-cognition (the soul) that departs (at death) — by what, and quitting which station, does it depart?' He answered: 'In the middle of the heart is a red lump of flesh, in which is that small lotus opened out like a water-lily; in its centre is an ocean, in the ocean's centre a sheath, and in it are four channels — Ramā, Aramā, Icchā, Apunarbhavā. Of these, Ramā by merit leads (the soul) to a meritorious world; Aramā by demerit to an evil one; by Icchā one attains whatever one remembers (at the end); by Apunarbhavā one pierces the sheath. Having pierced the sheath, one pierces the skull; the earth; then water, fire, air, ether, mind, the <em>mahat</em>, the unmanifest, the imperishable, and death — and death becomes one with the supreme God. Beyond is neither existent, nor non-existent, nor both. This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.'\"","para":"Raikva's third question concerns <strong>death</strong>: by what route does the departing soul (<em>vijñāna-ghana</em>, \"mass-of-cognition\") leave? Within the heart-lotus is an \"ocean\" and a \"sheath\" holding <strong>four channels</strong> that determine destiny: <strong>Ramā</strong> (merit → good world), <strong>Aramā</strong> (demerit → evil world), <strong>Icchā</strong> (last desire/memory → corresponding birth — cf. Gītā 8.6, <em>yaṃ yaṃ vāpi smaran bhāvam</em>), and <strong>Apunarbhavā</strong> (\"no-more-becoming\") — the channel of <strong>liberation</strong>. The liberated soul, taking the fourth, <strong>pierces sheath after sheath</strong> — skull, then the whole tattva-ladder up through earth → … → the unmanifest → the imperishable → <strong>death itself</strong> — until \"death becomes one with the supreme God,\" beyond which lies the unutterable. This is the <em>kapāla-bheda</em>/<em>brahma-randhra</em> exit of the yoga tradition, given a full metaphysical ascent.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the four nāḍīs as the karmically determined exits, <em>Apunarbhavā</em> being the <em>suṣumnā</em>/devayāna of the knower; the serial \"piercing\" is the dissolution of every <em>kośa</em> and tattva until only the <em>para deva</em> remains"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","takes the liberation-route as the <strong>arcirādi-mārga</strong> (the path of light) by which the released soul reaches the Lord; \"death becomes one with God\" = the cessation of saṃsāra in the soul's attainment of Him"],["Paul Deussen","compares the four nāḍīs and the <em>icchayā yat smarati</em> doctrine to Gītā 8 and the Chāndogya/Kauṣītaki death-paths"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","makes this the Upaniṣad's <strong>art of conscious dying</strong> (see Practical Steps)"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the four channels and the piercing-sequence"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 12","title":"Food from Nārāyaṇa: The Discipline of Eating","sa":["ॐ नारायणाद्वा अन्नमागतं पक्वं ब्रह्मलोके महासंवर्तके, पुनः पक्वमादित्ये, पुनः पक्वं क्रव्यादि, पुनः पक्वम् । जालकिलक्लिन्नं पर्युषितं पूतमन्नमयाचितमसंक्लृप्तमश्नीयात्, न कंचन याचेत ॥ इति द्वादशः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ nārāyaṇād vā annam āgataṃ pakvaṃ brahma-loke mahā-saṃvartake, punaḥ pakvam āditye, punaḥ pakvaṃ kravyādi, punaḥ pakvam | jāla-kila-klinnaṃ paryuṣitaṃ pūtam annam ayācitam asaṃklṛptam aśnīyāt, na kaṃcana yāceta ‖"],"words":[["nārāyaṇāt vā annam āgatam","abl. + nom.","\"food, verily, has come from Nārāyaṇa\""],["pakvaṃ brahma-loke mahā-saṃvartake","n. + loc.","\"cooked in the Brahmā-world, in the great (fire of) dissolution\""],["punaḥ pakvam āditye","loc.","\"cooked again in the sun\""],["punaḥ pakvaṃ kravyādi","nom.","\"cooked again (by) the flesh-eating (fire of digestion)\""],["jāla-kila-klinnaṃ","n.","\"moistened (as if) through a lattice / softened\" (obscure)"],["paryuṣitaṃ pūtam annam","n. acc.","\"stale (but) purified food\""],["ayācitam asaṃklṛptam aśnīyāt","acc. + opt.","\"let one eat (it) unbegged and unprepared (not specially cooked for oneself)\""],["na kaṃcana yāceta","opt.","\"let one beg of no one\""]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. Food, verily, has come from Nārāyaṇa: cooked in the Brahmā-world in the great dissolution-fire, cooked again in the sun, cooked again by the flesh-consuming (digestive fire), cooked again. (Such) food — softened, even stale, (yet) purified — let one eat unbegged and not specially prepared (for oneself); let one beg of nothing from anyone.\"","para":"A precept for the renunciate woven from the \"all is Nārāyaṇa\" theology: since <strong>all food ultimately comes from Nārāyaṇa</strong> — \"cooked\" successively in the cosmic fire, the sun, and the body's digestive fire — the ascetic should accept whatever food comes <strong>unsolicited and unprepared-for-oneself</strong> (the <em>ayācita</em>/<em>asaṃklṛpta</em> ideal of the wandering monk), even if plain or stale, and should <strong>beg from no one</strong>. Right understanding of food's divine source frees the eater from craving and from dependence.","coms":[["Sivananda / Krishnananda","reads this as the practical <em>bhikṣu</em>-discipline that follows from kh. 6: seeing every meal as Nārāyaṇa's <em>prasāda</em> dissolves both fastidiousness and beggary"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","glosses the \"three cookings\" as the cosmic, solar, and gastric <em>agnis</em> (cf. Garbha Up.'s three fires)"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","stresses the food-as-gift-of-the-Lord as an attitude of <em>śaraṇāgati</em> (surrender)"],["Paul Deussen","notes the obscure <em>jāla-kila-klinna</em> and reads the verse as a saṃnyāsa-rule appended to the metaphysics"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the eating-rule"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 13","title":"The Conduct of the Knower; the Hearts of the Elements","sa":["बाल्येन तिष्ठासेद् — बालस्वभावोऽसङ्गो निरवद्यो मौनेन पाण्डित्येन निरवधिकारतयोपलभ्येत । कैवल्यमुक्तं निगमनम् । प्रजापतिरुवाच — महत्पदं ज्ञात्वा वृक्षमूले वसेत कुचेलोऽसहाय एकाकी समाधिस्थः, आत्मकाम आप्तकामो निष्कामो जीर्णकामः । हस्तिनि सिंहे दंशे मशके नकुले सर्पराक्षसगन्धर्वे मृत्यो रूपाणि विदित्वा न बिभेति कुतश्चन । वृक्षमिव तिष्ठासेच्छिद्यमानोऽपि न कुप्येत न कम्पेत, उत्पलमिव ..., आकाशमिव तिष्ठासेच्छिद्यमानोऽपि न कुप्येत न कम्पेत । सत्येन तिष्ठासेत् — सत्योऽयमात्मा । सर्वेषामेव गन्धानां पृथिवी हृदयं, सर्वेषामेव रसानामापो हृदयं, सर्वेषामेव रूपाणां तेजो हृदयं, सर्वेषामेव स्पर्शानां वायुर्हृदयं, सर्वेषामेव शब्दानामाकाशं हृदयं, सर्वेषामेव गतीनामव्यक्तं हृदयं, सर्वेषामेव सत्त्वानां मृत्युर्हृदयम् — मृत्युर्वै परे देव एकीभवति । परस्तान्न सन्नासन्न सदसदित्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥ इति त्रयोदशः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["bālyena tiṣṭhāsed — bāla-svabhāvo 'saṅgo niravadyo maunena pāṇḍityena niravadhikāratayopalabhyeta | kaivalyam uktaṃ nigamanam | prajāpatir uvāca — mahat-padaṃ jñātvā vṛkṣa-mūle vaseta kucelo 'sahāya ekākī samādhi-sthaḥ, ātma-kāma āpta-kāmo niṣkāmo jīrṇa-kāmaḥ | hastini siṃhe daṃśe maśake nakule sarpa-rākṣasa-gandharve mṛtyo rūpāṇi viditvā na bibheti kutaścana | vṛkṣam iva tiṣṭhāsec chidyamāno 'pi na kupyeta na kampeta, utpalam iva …, ākāśam iva tiṣṭhāsec chidyamāno 'pi na kupyeta na kampeta | satyena tiṣṭhāset — satyo 'yam ātmā | sarveṣām eva gandhānāṃ pṛthivī hṛdayaṃ, sarveṣām eva rasānām āpo hṛdayaṃ, … sarveṣām eva sattvānāṃ mṛtyur hṛdayaṃ — mṛtyur vai pare deva ekī-bhavati ‖"],"words":[["bālyena tiṣṭhāset","instr. + opt.","\"let one remain in the state of a child (childlike innocence)\""],["bāla-svabhāvaḥ asaṅgaḥ niravadyaḥ","nom.","\"of childlike nature, unattached, blameless\""],["maunena pāṇḍityena niravadhikāratayā upalabhyeta","instr.","\"by silence, by (true) learning, by desirelessness-for-results, (the Self) is attained\""],["kaivalyam uktaṃ nigamanam","nom.","\"Aloneness (kaivalya) is declared (as) the conclusion\""],["mahat-padaṃ jñātvā vṛkṣa-mūle vaseta","acc. + abs. + opt.","\"knowing the great State, let one dwell at the root of a tree\""],["kucelaḥ asahāyaḥ ekākī samādhi-sthaḥ","nom.","\"ill-clad, helperless, alone, fixed in samādhi\""],["ātma-kāmaḥ āpta-kāmaḥ niṣkāmaḥ jīrṇa-kāmaḥ","nom.","\"desiring (only) the Self, his desires fulfilled, desireless, his desires worn out\""],["mṛtyoḥ rūpāṇi viditvā na bibheti","acc. + abs. + pres.","\"knowing (all these — elephant, lion, gnat, serpent…) as the forms of Death, he fears nothing\""],["vṛkṣam iva … chidyamānaḥ api na kupyeta na kampeta","nom.","\"like a tree, though cut, he would not be angered nor tremble\""],["utpalam iva … ākāśam iva","\"like a lotus… like space\"","\"like a lotus… like the sky (unmoved though struck)\""],["satyena tiṣṭhāset, satyaḥ ayam ātmā","instr. + nom.","\"let one abide by truth; this Self is truth\""],["sarveṣāṃ gandhānāṃ pṛthivī hṛdayam","gen. + nom.","\"earth is the heart (essence) of all smells\""],["…āpaḥ…tejaḥ…vāyuḥ…ākāśaṃ…avyaktaṃ…mṛtyuḥ hṛdayam","nom.","\"(so) water of tastes, fire of forms, air of touches, ether of sounds, the unmanifest of motions, death of (all) beings\""]],"lit":"\"Let one abide in childlikeness — of childlike nature, unattached, blameless; by silence, by (true) learning, and by desirelessness-for-results the Self is attained. Aloneness (<em>kaivalya</em>) is declared the conclusion. Prajāpati said: knowing the great State, let one dwell at the root of a tree, ill-clad, without helper, alone, fixed in samādhi — desiring the Self, his desires fulfilled, desireless, his desires worn out. Recognizing in the elephant, lion, gnat, mosquito, mongoose, serpent, Rākṣasa, and Gandharva (only) the forms of Death, he fears nothing whatever. Like a tree, though hewn, he would feel no anger and not tremble; like a lotus…; like space, though struck, he would feel no anger and not tremble. Let one abide by truth — this Self is truth. Earth is the heart of all smells; water of all tastes; fire of all forms; air of all touches; ether of all sounds; the unmanifest of all motions; death of all beings — and death becomes one with the supreme God…\"","para":"A portrait of the <strong>liberated sage's conduct</strong> (parallel to BĀU 3.5's <em>bālyena tiṣṭhāset</em> and the <em>muni</em> ideal). The knower lives <strong>childlike, unattached, silent, desireless</strong>, at a tree's root, alone in samādhi, his desires \"worn out\" (<em>jīrṇa-kāma</em>). Seeing every threatening creature as merely a <strong>form of Death</strong>, he is <strong>fearless</strong>; struck or cut, he is unmoved as a <strong>tree, a lotus, or space</strong>. He \"abides by truth, for this Self is truth.\" The khaṇḍa closes by naming each element the <em>hṛdaya</em> (\"heart,\" essence) of its proper sense-quality — earth of smell, water of taste, etc. — the series again terminating in <em>mṛtyu</em> and its merger in the supreme God.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads <em>bālya</em> not as childishness but as the egoless spontaneity of the <em>jīvanmukta</em> (the <em>bāla-unmatta-piśāca</em> conduct of later Advaita); <em>kaivalya</em> is the goal"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","would soften <em>kaivalya</em> toward communion with the Lord rather than bare isolation, and reads \"this Self is truth\" as the dependence of the soul on the <em>satya</em> that is Brahman"],["Paul Deussen","notes the BĀU 3.5 (<em>bālyena tiṣṭhāset</em>) and the elements-as-heart series"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","makes the tree/lotus/space similes a practical teaching on <em>titikṣā</em> (forbearance) and fearlessness"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the sage-portrait"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 14","title":"The Food and the Eater","sa":["ॐ पृथिवी वा अन्नम्, आपोऽन्नादाः; आपो वा अन्नं, ज्योतिरन्नादम्; ज्योतिर्वा अन्नं, वायुरन्नादः; वायुर्वा अन्नम्, आकाशोऽन्नादः; आकाशो वा अन्नम्, इन्द्रियाण्यन्नादानि; इन्द्रियाणि वा अन्नं, मनोऽन्नादम्; मनो वा अन्नं, बुद्धिरन्नादा; बुद्धिर्वा अन्नम्, अव्यक्तमन्नादम्; अव्यक्तं वा अन्नम्, अक्षरमन्नादम्; अक्षरं वा अन्नं, मृत्युरन्नादः — मृत्युर्वै परे देव एकीभवति । परस्तान्न सन्नासन्न सदसदित्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥ इति चतुर्दशः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ pṛthivī vā annam, āpo 'nnādāḥ; āpo vā annaṃ, jyotir annādam; jyotir vā annaṃ, vāyur annādaḥ; vāyur vā annam, ākāśo 'nnādaḥ; ākāśo vā annam, indriyāṇy annādāni; indriyāṇi vā annaṃ, mano 'nnādam; mano vā annaṃ, buddhir annādā; buddhir vā annam, avyaktam annādam; avyaktaṃ vā annam, akṣaram annādam; akṣaraṃ vā annaṃ, mṛtyur annādaḥ — mṛtyur vai pare deva ekī-bhavati ‖"],"words":[["pṛthivī vā annam","nom.","\"earth, verily, is food\""],["āpaḥ annādāḥ","nom.","\"water is the food-eater\""],["jyotiḥ / vāyuḥ / ākāśaḥ / indriyāṇi / manaḥ / buddhiḥ / avyaktam / akṣaram / mṛtyuḥ — annam … annādaḥ","nom.","\"(each in turn is) food … (and the next) the eater\""],["mṛtyuḥ annādaḥ","nom.","\"death is the (final) eater\""],["mṛtyuḥ vai pare deve ekī-bhavati","loc. + pres.","\"death becomes one with the supreme God\""]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. Earth is food, water the eater; water is food, fire the eater; fire is food, air the eater; air is food, ether the eater; ether is food, the senses the eaters; the senses are food, mind the eater; mind is food, intellect the eater; intellect is food, the unmanifest the eater; the unmanifest is food, the imperishable the eater; the imperishable is food, death the eater — and death becomes one with the supreme God. Beyond is neither existent, nor non-existent, nor both. This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.\"","para":"A <strong>food-chain of being</strong> (<em>anna/annāda</em>, \"food/eater,\" a favourite Upaniṣadic figure — cf. BĀU 1.4.6, Maitri 6.9–13): each principle is the \"food\" consumed by the next subtler one, in the same ascending tattva-order as the <em>laya</em> of kh. 2 and the death-ascent of kh. 11 — earth → … → senses → mind → intellect → unmanifest → imperishable → <strong>death</strong>, the last and universal eater, which itself <strong>merges in the supreme God</strong>. The series dramatizes that every level of reality is provisional, \"consumed\" by the next, until all is resolved in the One beyond <em>sat</em> and <em>asat</em>.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the <em>anna/annāda</em> ladder as the hierarchy of <em>grāhya/grāhaka</em> (object/subject), each subsumed by the subtler, culminating in the Self that is neither"],["Paul Deussen","links it to the <em>anna</em> speculations of BĀU and Maitri"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","takes \"death merges in the supreme God\" as all transience resolving into the Lord who alone is <em>amṛta</em>"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","uses the ladder as a contemplative <em>neti</em> — \"I am not this 'food,' nor even its 'eater,' but That in which both end.\""],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the chain"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 15","title":"What the Departing Soul \"Burns\"","sa":["अथ हैनं रैक्वः पप्रच्छ — भगवन्योऽयं विज्ञानघन उत्क्रामन्स केन कतरद्वा स्थानं दहतीति । तस्मै स होवाच — योऽयं विज्ञानघन उत्क्रामन्प्राणं दहत्यपानं व्यानमुदानं समानं वैरम्भं मुख्यमन्तर्यामं प्रभञ्जनं कुमारं श्येनं श्वेतं कृष्णं नागं दहति; पृथिव्यापस्तेजोवायुराकाशं दहति; जागरितं स्वप्नं सुषुप्तं तुरीयं च महान्तं च लोकं परं च लोकं दहति; लोकालोकं दहति; धर्माधर्मं दहति; अभास्करममर्यादं निरालोकमतः परं दहति; महान्तं दहति, अव्यक्तं दहति, अक्षरं दहति, मृत्युं दहति — मृत्युर्वै परे देव एकीभवति । परस्तान्न सन्नासन्न सदसदित्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥ इति पञ्चदशः खण्डः ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ raikvaḥ papraccha — bhagavan yo 'yaṃ vijñāna-ghana utkrāman sa kena kataradvā sthānaṃ dahatīti | tasmai sa hovāca — yo 'yaṃ vijñāna-ghana utkrāman prāṇaṃ dahaty apānaṃ vyānam udānaṃ samānaṃ vairambhaṃ mukhyam antaryāmaṃ prabhañjanaṃ kumāraṃ śyenaṃ śvetaṃ kṛṣṇaṃ nāgaṃ dahati; pṛthivy-āpas-tejo-vāyur-ākāśaṃ dahati; jāgaritaṃ svapnaṃ suṣuptaṃ turīyaṃ ca mahāntaṃ ca lokaṃ paraṃ ca lokaṃ dahati; lokālokaṃ dahati; dharmādharmaṃ dahati; abhāskaram amaryādaṃ nirālokam ataḥ paraṃ dahati; mahāntaṃ dahati, avyaktaṃ dahati, akṣaraṃ dahati, mṛtyuṃ dahati — mṛtyur vai pare deva ekī-bhavati ‖"],"words":[["yaḥ ayaṃ vijñāna-ghanaḥ utkrāman","nom.","\"this mass-of-cognition, departing\""],["kena kataradvā sthānaṃ dahati","instr. + acc.","\"by what, and which station, does it burn (up)?\""],["prāṇaṃ … apānaṃ vyānam udānaṃ samānaṃ vairambhaṃ mukhyam antaryāmaṃ prabhañjanaṃ … dahati","acc.","\"it burns up the (vital) breaths — prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna, vairambha, mukhya, antaryāma, prabhañjana…\""],["kumāraṃ śyenaṃ śvetaṃ kṛṣṇaṃ nāgaṃ dahati","acc.","\"…(the currents/serpents) Kumāra, Śyena, Śveta, Kṛṣṇa, Nāga\""],["pṛthivy-āpas-tejas-vāyus-ākāśaṃ dahati","acc.","\"it burns up earth, water, fire, air, ether\""],["jāgaritaṃ svapnaṃ suṣuptaṃ turīyaṃ ca dahati","acc.","\"it burns up waking, dream, deep sleep, and the Fourth (as objects)\""],["lokālokaṃ dharmādharmaṃ dahati","acc.","\"it burns up world-and-non-world, merit-and-demerit\""],["abhāskaram amaryādaṃ nirālokam ataḥ paraṃ dahati","acc.","\"it burns up the sunless, boundless, lightless (region) beyond\""],["mahāntaṃ … avyaktaṃ … akṣaraṃ … mṛtyuṃ dahati","acc.","\"it burns up the mahat, the unmanifest, the imperishable, death\""]],"lit":"\"Then Raikva asked him: 'Lord, this mass-of-cognition that departs — by what, and which station, does it burn up?' He answered: 'This mass-of-cognition, departing, burns up the breaths — prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna, vairambha, mukhya, antaryāma, prabhañjana — and (the currents) Kumāra, Śyena, Śveta, Kṛṣṇa, Nāga; it burns up earth, water, fire, air, ether; it burns up waking, dream, deep sleep, and the Fourth (as objects); it burns up world-and-non-world; it burns up merit-and-demerit; it burns up the sunless, boundless, lightless (region) beyond that; it burns up the <em>mahat</em>, the unmanifest, the imperishable, and death — and death becomes one with the supreme God. Beyond is neither existent, nor non-existent, nor both. This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda.'\"","para":"Raikva's fourth question recasts the death-ascent of kh. 11 in the idiom of <strong>\"burning\"</strong> (<em>dahati</em>): the departing knower's <em>vijñāna-ghana</em> <strong>consumes</strong>, one after another, the whole apparatus of conditioned existence — the vital winds and nāḍī-currents, the five elements, the <strong>four states</strong> (waking/dream/sleep/turīya as objects of knowledge), world and non-world, merit and demerit, even the \"lightless region beyond,\" and finally the <em>mahat</em>, the unmanifest, the imperishable, and <strong>death itself</strong> — leaving only the supreme God beyond <em>sat</em> and <em>asat</em>. Where kh. 11 \"pierced\" the sheaths, kh. 15 \"burns\" them: liberation as the <em>jñānāgni</em> (fire of knowledge, Gītā 4.37) that reduces all conditioning to ash.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads <em>dahati</em> as the <em>jñānāgni</em> of Gītā 4.37 (<em>jñānāgniḥ sarva-karmāṇi bhasmasāt kurute</em>): knowledge incinerates every tattva and even death, leaving the <em>para deva</em>"],["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","takes the \"burning\" as the soul's final shedding of all <em>prakṛti</em>-bonds on the path to the Lord"],["Paul Deussen","notes the parallel structure with kh. 11 and 14 (the same tattva-ladder, three idioms: dissolving, eating, burning)"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","reads it as the culmination of the art of dying — the conscious reduction of all that is not the Self"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the burning-sequence"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 16","title":"Secrecy, the Worthy Recipient, and Devotion","sa":["सौबालबीजब्रह्मोपनिषन्न प्रशान्ताय दातव्या, न अपुत्राय, न अशिष्याय, न असंवत्सररात्रोषिताय, न अपरिज्ञातकुलशीलाय दातव्या, नैव च प्रवक्तव्या । यस्य देवे परा भक्तिर्यथा देवे तथा गुरौ — तस्यैते कथिता ह्यर्थाः प्रकाशन्ते महात्मनः — इत्येतन्निर्वाणानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनमिति वेदानुशासनम् ॥","ॐ पूर्णमदः पूर्णमिदं पूर्णात्पूर्णमुदच्यते । पूर्णस्य पूर्णमादाय पूर्णमेवावशिष्यते ॥ ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥ इति षोडशः खण्डः ॥","इति सुबालोपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥"],"iast":["saubāla-bīja-brahmopaniṣan na praśāntāya dātavyā, na aputrāya, na aśiṣyāya, na asaṃvatsara-rātroṣitāya, na aparijñāta-kula-śīlāya dātavyā, naiva ca pravaktavyā | yasya deve parā bhaktir yathā deve tathā gurau — tasyaite kathitā hy arthāḥ prakāśante mahātmanaḥ — ity etan nirvāṇānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam iti vedānuśāsanam ‖ oṃ pūrṇam adaḥ pūrṇam idaṃ … pūrṇam evāvaśiṣyate | oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ‖"],"words":[["saubāla-bīja-brahma-upaniṣat","nom.","\"this Subāla seed-Brahman-Upaniṣad\""],["na praśāntāya dātavyā","dat. + gerundive","\"is not to be given to one who is not tranquil\""],["na aputrāya","dat.","\"not to one without a (worthy) son (= a true successor)\""],["na aśiṣyāya","dat.","\"not to one who is not a (proper) pupil\""],["na asaṃvatsara-rātra-uṣitāya","dat.","\"not to one who has not stayed (with the teacher) a year (of nights)\""],["na aparijñāta-kula-śīlāya","dat.","\"not to one whose family and character are unknown\""],["naiva ca pravaktavyā","gerundive","\"and it is not to be proclaimed (publicly)\""],["yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ","gen. + loc.","\"he who has supreme devotion to God\""],["yathā deve tathā gurau","\"as to God, so to the guru\"","\"(and) to the guru as to God\""],["tasya ete kathitāḥ arthāḥ prakāśante","gen. + nom. + pres.","\"to that great soul, these declared truths shine forth\""]],"lit":"\"This Subāla seed-Brahman-Upaniṣad is not to be given to one who is not tranquil, nor to one without a (true) successor, nor to a non-pupil, nor to one who has not dwelt (with the teacher for) a year of nights, nor to one whose lineage and character are unknown; and it is not to be proclaimed (openly). 'He who has supreme devotion to God, and as to God so to his guru — to that great soul these declared truths shine forth.' This is the instruction-for-Nirvāṇa, the instruction of the Veda, the instruction of the Veda. Oṃ. That is full, this is full… the full alone remains. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace. Thus the Subāla Upaniṣad is concluded.\"","para":"The closing <em>adhikāra</em> (eligibility) and secrecy clause, standard for the Upaniṣads: the teaching may be given <strong>only</strong> to the tranquil, tested, year-resident, well-known pupil with a worthy successor — never broadcast. The capstone is the famous verse <strong>shared verbatim with Śvetāśvatara 6.23</strong>: <em>yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ</em> — \"to him who has supreme <strong>devotion</strong> to God, and to his guru as to God, these truths shine forth.\" Strikingly, this <em>jñāna</em>-dense Upaniṣad ends by making <strong>bhakti</strong> the condition of revelation. The work then closes by repeating the <em>pūrṇam adaḥ</em> śānti with which it opened — fullness returning to fullness.","coms":[["Rāmānuja / Raṅgarāmānuja","prizes the closing verse as scriptural warrant that"],["bhakti","(to the Lord and, equally, to the guru) is the indispensable means of saving knowledge — the very Śvetāśvatara verse Rāmānuja cites elsewhere; the <em>arthāḥ prakāśante</em> (\"the truths shine forth,\" by grace) confirms revelation as the Lord's gift to the devotee"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the verse as requiring <em>bhakti</em> as the purifier that fits one for <em>jñāna</em>, but locates final release in knowledge"],["Paul Deussen","notes the Śvetāśvatara 6.23 borrowing and the conventional secrecy-formula"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","stresses <em>yathā deve tathā gurau</em> — equal devotion to God and guru — as the practical key"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the eligibility-rules and the closing verse"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Synthesis","title":"Details across the whole Upaniṣad","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>A compilation with a spine.</strong> Subāla is the most overtly <em>anthological</em> of the Upaniṣads, stitching together the Nāsadīya, the Puruṣa-sūkta, the Nārāyaṇa-/Viṣṇu-sūkta material, the antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa (BĀU 3.7), the dahara/nāḍī and three-states teaching (Chāndogya 8, BĀU 4), the <em>śānto dānto</em> and <em>na pravacanena</em> verses (BĀU 4.4 / Kaṭha 1.2), Gārgī's regress (BĀU 3.6), and Śvetāśvatara 6.23. Yet a clear architecture runs through it: <strong>cosmogony → reabsorption → the Self in the body and behind the organs → the Lord (Nārāyaṇa/antaryāmin) → the four dialogues of Raikva on reabsorption, ground, dying, and the burning of conditioning → the renunciate's life → devotion.</strong>","- <strong>Three idioms for one dissolution.</strong> The same ascending tattva-ladder (elements → senses → mind → mahat → avyakta → akṣara → mṛtyu → <em>para deva</em>) is run <strong>three times</strong> under three verbs: <em>vilīyate</em> (\"dissolves,\" kh. 2 — cosmic <em>pralaya</em>), <em>annāda</em> (\"is eaten,\" kh. 14 — the food-chain of being), and <em>dahati</em> (\"is burnt,\" kh. 15 — the fire of knowledge at death). Together with the \"piercing\" (<em>bhinatti</em>) of kh. 11, Subāla gives four complementary phenomenologies of return-to-the-One.","- <strong>The Advaita–Viśiṣṭādvaita seam.</strong> Subāla's \"<em>eko nārāyaṇaḥ</em>\" (kh. 6) and antaryāmin (kh. 7) are read by <strong>BY</strong> as the one nirguṇa Self under a name, and by <strong>Rāmānuja</strong> as the personal Lord whose <em>body</em> is all souls and worlds (<em>śarīra-śarīrin</em>). The closing <em>bhakti</em> verse (kh. 16) and the pervasive \"Nārāyaṇa\" naming tilt the text toward the theistic reading — which is exactly why it became a Śrī-Vaiṣṇava proof-text.","- <strong>The four destiny-channels (kh. 11).</strong> Ramā (merit), Aramā (demerit), Icchā (last-thought → rebirth), and Apunarbhavā (liberation) give a compact map of post-mortem destiny, with the last as the yogic <em>brahma-randhra</em> exit and metaphysical ascent.","- <strong>Jñāna framed by ethics and bhakti.</strong> For all its metaphysics, Subāla insists the Self is <strong>not</strong> won by learning, Vedas, sacrifice, or even Sāṃkhya-Yoga (kh. 9), but only by the <strong>tranquil, controlled, forbearing</strong> seeker (kh. 9), living <strong>childlike and fearless</strong> (kh. 13), eating <strong>what comes unbidden</strong> (kh. 12), with <strong>supreme devotion to God and guru</strong> (kh. 16)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>SUBĀLA UPANIṢAD — ARCHITECTURE OF THE SIXTEEN KHAṆḌAS\n\n  kh.1–2  COSMOGONY ↓ and REABSORPTION ↑\n          &quot;na san na asat&quot; → tamas → bhūtādi → ākāśa → vāyu →\n          agni → āpas → pṛthivī → EGG → PURUṢA (sahasraśīrṣā)\n          → varṇas &amp; organs … then VILĪYATE back up to PARA DEVA\n\n  kh.3    APOPHATIC BRAHMAN — asad vā idam; neti-neti;\n          won by satya·dāna·tapas·brahmacarya + dama·dāna·dayā;\n          &quot;brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti&quot; (no departure at death)\n\n  kh.4    THE HEART — dahara lotus · 10 apertures · breaths &amp; visions ·\n          100 nāḍīs ×72,000 · hitā channels · SAMPRASĀDA (deep sleep) · salila\n\n  kh.5    THE SELF BEHIND EVERY ORGAN   ┌ adhyātma (organ)\n          14 faculties, each:            ┤ adhibhūta (object)\n          → channel→breath→vijñāna→      └ adhidaivata (deity)\n            ānanda→hṛd-ākāśa → ĀTMAN = antaryāmī (Māṇḍūkya-7 close)\n\n  kh.6    ★ ALL IS NĀRĀYAṆA ★ — eko nārāyaṇaḥ; every organ/object/\n          god/Veda/relation = Nārāyaṇa → &quot;tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam&quot;\n\n  kh.7    ★ ANTARYĀMIN ★ — &quot;yasya pṛthivī śarīraṃ … yaṃ pṛthivī na veda&quot;\n          (12 bodies: 5 elements + manas/buddhi/ahaṃkāra/citta +\n           avyakta/akṣara/mṛtyu) = sarvabhūtāntarātmā = NĀRĀYAṆA\n          lineage: Apāntaratamas→Brahmā→Ghorāṅgiras→Raikva→Rāma→all\n\n  kh.8    PURE SELF in IMPURE BODY — painted-wall · mirage-city ·\n          plantain-pith · water-bubble → &quot;vidvāṃsaḥ paśyanti&quot;\n\n  ── RAIKVA&#x27;S FOUR DIALOGUES ──────────────────────────────\n  kh.9   &quot;in what do all SET?&quot;      → reabsorb → NIRBĪJA\n                                      (Kaṭha 1.2.23 + BĀU 4.4.23)\n  kh.10  &quot;in what are all FIXED?&quot;   → worlds woven in the Self\n                                      &quot;like gems on a thread&quot;\n  kh.11  &quot;how does the soul DIE?&quot;   → 4 nāḍīs (Ramā/Aramā/Icchā/\n                                      Apunarbhavā) → pierce the sheaths\n  kh.15  &quot;what does it BURN?&quot;       → jñānāgni consumes all tattvas\n  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n  kh.12  FOOD from Nārāyaṇa — eat the unbegged, unprepared; beg nothing\n  kh.13  THE SAGE — childlike·silent·fearless; tree/lotus/space unmoved;\n         each element = the &quot;heart&quot; of its sense-quality\n  kh.14  FOOD &amp; EATER ladder — earth→…→akṣara→MṚTYU → para deva\n\n  kh.16  SECRECY &amp; ★ BHAKTI ★ — &quot;yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ&quot; (= Śvet 6.23)\n         → closes with PŪRṆAM ADAḤ (full ↔ full)\n\n  THREE IDIOMS OF RETURN:  vilīyate (dissolve, k2) · annāda (eaten, k14)\n                            · dahati (burnt, k15) · bhinatti (pierced, k11)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps and observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>The reabsorption meditation (kh. 2, 9, 11, 14, 15).</strong> Subāla's central practice is <em>laya-cintana</em>: trace experience inward and \"upward,\" dissolving the gross into the subtle — body → senses → mind → intellect → the unmanifest → the imperishable → the One beyond <em>sat</em> and <em>asat</em>. Run it in any of the text's four idioms (dissolving, eating, burning, piercing); each is a complete contemplative ascent.","2. <strong>The seven-step inward trace (kh. 5).</strong> For any single perception, follow Subāla's chain: from the <strong>organ</strong> to its <strong>object</strong>, to the cosmic <strong>deity</strong>, to the <strong>channel</strong>, the <strong>breath</strong>, <strong>cognition</strong>, <strong>bliss</strong>, and the <strong>heart-ether</strong> — and rest in the Self that \"moves within this whole series.\" This converts ordinary sensing into a ladder to the witness.","3. <strong>See the antaryāmin (kh. 7).</strong> Take each layer in turn — earth, water, … mind, intellect, ego, the unmanifest, death — and contemplate: <em>whose body is this, who dwells within it, whom it knows not.</em> The discipline locates the one indweller behind every sheath of world and psyche.","4. <strong>Childlike, tree-like, fearless (kh. 13).</strong> Cultivate <em>bālya</em> (egoless simplicity), <em>mauna</em> (silence), and the equanimity of the <strong>tree, lotus, and space</strong> — \"though cut, neither angered nor trembling.\" Practise seeing whatever threatens as merely \"a form of Death,\" dissolving fear.","5. <strong>Eat as Nārāyaṇa's gift (kh. 12).</strong> Receive food as already \"cooked by Nārāyaṇa\" in the cosmic, solar, and digestive fires; take what comes <strong>unbegged and unprepared-for-self</strong>, without fastidiousness or beggary — a daily exercise in surrender and contentment.","6. <strong>The art of dying (kh. 11, 15).</strong> Subāla is, among other things, a manual for conscious death: know the four heart-channels, and cultivate now the <strong>Apunarbhavā</strong> disposition (no-more-becoming) so that, at the end, attention takes the liberating route — recalling that \"whatever one remembers (by <em>Icchā</em>), that one attains.\"","7. <strong>Knowledge ripened by tranquillity and devotion (kh. 9, 16).</strong> Hold together the two framing teachings: the Self is <em>not</em> won by mere learning or technique (kh. 9) but by becoming <strong>śānta-dānta-uparata-titikṣu-samāhita</strong>; and the truths \"shine forth\" only to one with <strong>supreme devotion to God and guru</strong> (kh. 16). Discipline and devotion are the soil in which Subāla's knowledge germinates from \"seed\" (<em>bīja</em>) into the \"seedless\" (<em>nirbīja</em>)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Bibliography","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>Primary text &amp; translations</strong>","- <em>Subālopaniṣat</em>, ITRANS e-text `subAla.itx` (transcr. Sunder Hattangadi), sanskritdocuments.org — base text for this edition.","- <strong>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar</strong>, <em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em> (Madras, 1914) — English translation of Subāla with notes.","- <strong>Paul Deussen</strong>, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (Leipzig, 1897); Eng. tr. Bedekar &amp; Palsule, <em>Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda</em> (Delhi, 1980) — <em>Subāla-Upaniṣad</em> with introduction and notes.","- <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong>, <em>Dīpikā</em> on the 108 Upaniṣads, ed. Adyar Library — the sole classical Sanskrit commentary.","<strong>Viśiṣṭādvaita</strong>","- <strong>Rāmānuja</strong>, <em>Śrī-Bhāṣya</em> (on Brahma-Sūtra), with its citations of Subāla for the <em>antaryāmin</em> and <em>sarva-bhūtāntarātmā</em>; tr. G. Thibaut (SBE 48) / M. R. Sampatkumaran.","- <strong>Raṅgarāmānuja</strong>, <em>prakāśikā</em> commentaries on the minor Upaniṣads (Śrī-Vaiṣṇava tradition).","<strong>Modern Vedānta</strong>","- <strong>Swami Sivananda / Swami Krishnananda</strong> (Divine Life Society), writings and lectures on the Upaniṣads — thematic Vedāntic reading; Krishnananda's <em>The Realisation of the Absolute</em> draws on the Subāla cosmology."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references within the Upaniṣadic & Vedic corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Subāla locus</td><td>Parallel / source</td><td>Note</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 1 <em>na san na asat</em></td><td>Ṛgveda 10.129 (Nāsadīya-sūkta)</td><td>the indeterminate origin</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 1 <em>tamas → bhūtādi → ākāśa…</em></td><td>Sāṃkhya-Kārikā 22; Maitri 1</td><td>the guṇa/tattva emanation</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 1 <em>sahasraśīrṣā puruṣaḥ</em>; varṇas &amp; organs</td><td>Ṛgveda 10.90 (Puruṣa-sūkta)</td><td>verbatim cosmic-Person material</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 2 \"breath = the Vedas\"; <em>ardha strī ardha puruṣaḥ</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 2.4.10; 1.4.3</td><td>the Great Being's exhalation; self-division</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 2 <em>laya</em>-ladder</td><td>Maitri 6.10; Sāṃkhya</td><td>reabsorption of the 24 tattvas</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 3 <em>asad vā idam</em>; <em>aśabdam…</em></td><td>Taittirīya 2.7; Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.8.8</td><td>the apophatic Brahman</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 3 <em>dama-dāna-dayā</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 5.2 (<em>da-da-da</em>)</td><td>the three disciplines</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 3 <em>na tasya prāṇā utkrāmanti</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.6</td><td>the knower's death-without-transit</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 4 dahara, 72,000 nāḍīs, <em>samprasāda</em>, <em>salila</em></td><td>Chāndogya 8.1, 8.6; Bṛhadāraṇyaka 2.1.19, 4.3</td><td>heart-cave &amp; deep-sleep self</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 5 <em>adhyātma/adhibhūta/adhidaivata</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.5; Bhagavad-Gītā 8.1–4</td><td>the triple correlation</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 5 <em>nāntaḥprajño…</em></td><td>Māṇḍūkya 7</td><td>the turīya by negation</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 6 <em>eko nārāyaṇaḥ</em></td><td>Mahānārāyaṇa-Up.; Nārāyaṇa-sūkta</td><td>the Nārāyaṇa theology</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 6 <em>puruṣa evedaṃ sarvam</em>; <em>tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam</em></td><td>ṚV 10.90.2; ṚV 1.22.20–21</td><td>the Viṣṇu/Puruṣa theophany verses</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 7 <em>yasya pṛthivī śarīraṃ… yaṃ pṛthivī na veda</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.7 (antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa)</td><td>the inner-controller doctrine</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 8 painted-wall / mirage / plantain / bubble</td><td>Maitri 4.2</td><td>shared <em>māyā</em>-similes</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 9 <em>naivam ātmā pravacanena labhyate</em></td><td>Kaṭha 1.2.23; Muṇḍaka 3.2.3</td><td>the Self not won by mere means</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 9 <em>śānto dānto uparatas titikṣuḥ samāhitaḥ</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.23</td><td>the qualified knower</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 10 <em>ota-prota</em> regress of worlds</td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.6 (Gārgī)</td><td>\"warp and woof\"</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 11 <em>icchayā yat smarati</em></td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 8.5–6</td><td>last-thought determines rebirth</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 11 piercing the sheaths / <em>brahma-randhra</em></td><td>Kaṭha 2.3.16 (101st nāḍī); Yoga texts</td><td>the cranial exit</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 14 <em>anna / annāda</em> ladder</td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.4.6; Maitri 6.9–13; Taittirīya 2 (annamaya)</td><td>food-and-eater hierarchy</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 15 <em>dahati</em> (jñānāgni)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 4.37</td><td>the fire of knowledge</td></tr><tr><td>kh. 16 <em>yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 6.23</td><td>bhakti as the condition of revelation</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Thus the complete Subāla Upaniṣad — the cosmogony and reabsorption (1–2); the apophatic Brahman (3); the heart-lotus and the self of sleep (4); the Self behind every organ (5); the all-pervading Nārāyaṇa (6) and the antaryāmin (7); the pure Self in the impure body (8); Raikva's four dialogues on reabsorption, the world-in-the-Self, dying, and the burning of conditioning (9–11, 15); the food-discipline (12); the conduct of the knower (13); the food-and-eater ladder (14); and the closing teaching on secrecy and devotion (16) — the</em> nirvāṇānuśāsanam, <em>the instruction of the Veda, concluded. Oṃ pūrṇam adaḥ pūrṇam idaṃ… Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ.</em>"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
